- Home office: because you’re self-employed for the contracting work, you can take a home-office deduction (either simplified or actual-expense method). Actual-expense method using your rent will very likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest: the maximum federal adjustment is $2,500, but it phases out based on MAGI. With your W-2 plus net self-employment income, that deduction will likely be reduced or eliminated. You need the exact MAGI to be sure.
- Charitable: your $2,000 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize. If your total itemized deductions (charity + state/local taxes + mortgage interest + medical, etc.) do not exceed the standard deduction, take the standard deduction.
- Recommendation (based on the information you gave): You’ll probably take the standard deduction for federal tax, but still claim business deductions (including the home-office share) on Schedule C because those reduce your self-employment/net business income before computing AGI. Consider maximizing retirement (SEP IRA, solo 401(k)) or other above-the-line options if you want to reduce AGI.
