- Actual-expense method (likely larger):
  - Home-office share of rent: 14.49% × $28,800 = about $4,174
  - If you have other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, maintenance) their home-office share can be added — you didn’t list them, so I used rent only.
- Because your actual share of rent (~$4,174) is much larger than the $500 simplified amount, actual-expense method will likely be better. To claim actual you generally file Form 8829 (or report on Schedule C where appropriate). You must use the space regularly and exclusively for business and be the principal place of business.

Self-employment and related taxes
- Report the $15,000 gross on Schedule C and subtract allowable business expenses (home-office share, $650 other expenses, and any other business costs).
- That produces net self-employment income. Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is then calculated on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of your self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Example (rounded, approximate):
  - Home-office (actual): ≈ $4,174
  - Other business expenses: $650
  - Net self-employment income ≈ $15,000 − $4,824 = $10,176
  - Self-employment tax (approx 15.3% on 92.35% of net) ≈ $1,440
  - Half SE tax deduction (adjustment) ≈ $720
- These business deductions reduce your AGI even if you take the standard deduction for itemizing purposes.

Student loan interest
- Federal student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 (an adjustment to income) but phases out based on MAGI. For single filers the phaseout range (recent years) started around $75k and phased out by about $90k (these exact thresholds are inflation-adjusted each year).
- With W-2 wages of $84,900 and the self-employment net income above, your MAGI/AGI is likely over the phaseout limit, so the student loan interest deduction will likely be partly or fully disallowed. We can compute this precisely with exact numbers and the IRS MAGI thresholds for tax year 2024. Also, only interest paid in the tax year is relevant and you should have Form 1098-E from your loan servicer (MOHELA).

Charitable donations
- Cash gift $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. It does not reduce AGI directly (except for certain pandemic/temporary rules that no longer apply). Compare total itemized deductions (charity + state/local tax up to $10k SALT cap + mortgage interest + other eligible items) to the standard deduction to choose the best option.

W-2 unreimbursed expenses
- If the $650 are unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses for your TechStart job, those are no longer deductible on federal returns (suspended through 2025 by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act). If those expenses relate to the contracting/self-employment, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

Which forms you’ll need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments such as student loan interest, half of SE tax)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report contracting income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use actual-expense method for home office (optional; simplified method does not use 8829)
  - Schedule A (only if you itemize)
  - 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC as received from clients — use them to reconcile income; you must report all income even if no 1099 is issued
  - Form 1098-E (student loan interest) from MOHELA — for your records and to support a student-loan-interest deduction
- California:
  - CA Form 540 (resident return) and state schedules — California generally taxes business income and will have its own adjustments; California’s conformity to federal adjustments may differ in some respects (verify with CA tax instructions or a preparer).

Estimated tax / payments
- Because you have self-employment income, you may owe self-employment tax and additional income tax. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file, you should make quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties. Consider adjusting withholding on your W-2 job if possible to cover tax on your contracting income.

Records to gather (before you file)
- W-2 from TechStart Inc.
- 1099-NEC / records of the $15,000 contracting income (bank deposits, invoices) — report all income even if no 1099 was issued.
