Length: 9126 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up-front
- Report the $15,000 contracting income on Schedule C (you’re self‑employed for that income), pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can take a home‑office deduction for the 100 sq ft used exclusively for the contracting work.
- The $2,000 gift to Northwest Harvest is only deductible if you itemize. With your income and limited other itemizable items, you will very likely be better off taking the 2024 standard deduction for single filers (standard deduction >> $2,000 charitable).
- The student‑loan interest deduction only applies to interest you paid in the tax year being filed and is phased out at higher incomes — based on your wages + contracting income, you will likely be over the phaseout and not eligible for the student interest deduction for 2024 (see clarification request below).
- Key federal forms you will need: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (business use of home) if you use the regular method for home office, and Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) if you claim the 20% QBI deduction. If you itemize, use Schedule A. State: California Form 540 (and Schedule CA).

Detailed explanation, calculations, and required clarifying items

1) Which amounts are reported where
- W‑2 wages ($84,900): report on Form 1040 (Box 1 of W‑2).
- Contracting income ($15,000): report on Schedule C (gross receipts). Any ordinary and necessary expenses directly related to that work reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Bank interest ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115): report as interest income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1, if applicable).
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest): deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize; keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650): these are deductible only if they are business expenses for your self‑employment (Schedule C). If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Student loan interest ($3,600): only interest paid in the 2024 tax year may be deductible (up to $2,500) as an adjustment to income on Form 1040, subject to income phaseouts. You should have received Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer for interest paid in 2024. Note: listing interest you paid in prior years (2016–2019) does not create a current deduction — only payments in the year you file count.

2) Home office: how to treat it and which method to use
- You said the home office is 100 sq ft exclusive and your total is 690 sq ft. That’s ~14.49% of the home.
- You pay $2,400/month rent = $28,800/year. Allocating 14.49% of rent gives about $4,176/year attributable to the office.
- Two ways to calculate the home office deduction:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Regular method: pro‑rata share of actual expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). With rent alone you get ~ $4,176 (likely larger than $500). Use Form 8829 to calculate the regular method deduction for self‑employed taxpayers.
- Important: home office deduction is available for self‑employed/contractors on Schedule C. If you used the home office for a W‑2 job (and are an employee), you generally cannot take the home office deduction.
- Recommendation: if the office is used only for your contracting clients (not for your W‑2 job) you will almost certainly get a larger deduction with the regular method given your rent; use Form 8829 and keep documentation (lease, rent payments, utilities, photos showing exclusive use).

3) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Your Schedule C net income (gross 15,000 minus business expenses such as the $650 and the home office allocation) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). Rough estimate:
  - Example (regular home office): business expenses = $650 + $4,176 = $4,826 → net business income ≈ $10,174.
  - SE tax = 15.3% of ~92.35% of net self‑employment income ≈ $1,400–$1,500 (you get to deduct half of this on Form 1040).
- You may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) on up to 20% of your qualified business income from the contracting activity; in the example above that’s roughly $2,000. The QBI deduction reduces taxable income (use Form 8995 or 8995‑A).
- Because of SE tax and potential under‑withholding, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties.

4) Standard deduction vs itemize (federal)
- 2024 standard deduction for single filers is approximately $14,600. (Confirm exact IRS figure in current year materials.)
- Your likely federal itemizable deductions appear small: $2,000 charitable + possibly state income tax paid + mortgage interest (you have none) + other deductible expenses. Unless you have substantial additional deductible amounts (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over the AGI threshold), the standard deduction will be larger.
- So: you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for federal 2024.

5) Student loan interest deduction — eligibility check
- Two key points:
  - Only interest actually paid in the 2024 tax year qualifies. Clarify: is the $3,600 the amount of interest you paid in 2024? Or are those totals for earlier years (2016–2019)? If those payments are from past years, they do not give you a current deduction.
  - The deduction is phased out for higher incomes. For single filers the 2023 phaseout started around $75k and was gone above $90k. With your combined income (W‑2 + net self‑employment + interest), you are likely over the 2024 applicable phaseout threshold and therefore ineligible for the student loan interest deduction. I recommend checking the 2024 phaseout limits or I can check them for you if you want me to look up the current threshold.

6) California state tax
- File California Form 540. California generally follows federal treatment for business income and home office for self‑employment, but there are conforming and non‑conforming items — check the CA instructions or consult a preparer. CA standard deduction is much smaller than federal (so itemizing rules will differ); still, a $2,000 charitable gift alone will almost certainly not push you above CA itemized threshold versus CA standard deduction.

7) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 and your business expenses
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using regular method for home office
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction), if applicable
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) as needed (interest, adjustments)
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC (if clients issued them), W‑2, 1098‑E (student loan interest) if available

8) Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable gift(s) (your receipt for the $2,000).
- 1098‑E from MOHELA for any student loan interest paid in 2024.
- Records of rent payments, utilities, photos/layout showing the exclusive home office space, and any bills for utilities/rent (for Form 8829).
- Records/receipts for the $650 business expenses (if they are business expenses).

9) Action items / clarifying questions for you
Please confirm:
- Did you actually pay $3,600 of student loan interest during 2024, or is that an aggregate from earlier years (2016–2019)? (Only 2024 payments count for the 2024 return.)
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your contracting work, or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
- Is the 100 sq ft space used exclusively for the contracting business (not used for personal or W‑2 work)?
- Do you want me to run a more precise estimated tax calculation (federal tax, SE tax, and estimated payments) using either the simplified or regular home‑office method? If yes, I can produce a line‑by‑line estimate.

10) Final recommendation
- If the home office is exclusively for the contracting work and the $650 are business expenses, use the regular home‑office method (Form 8829) because your allocated rent will likely exceed the simplified $500 amount.
- Report contracting income on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and consider QBI (Form 8995).
- Expect to take the federal standard deduction for 2024 (do not itemize just for the $2,000 charity).
- Check the student loan interest eligibility carefully — likely phased out at your income level; only 2024 interest payments are deductible.
- Consider making or increasing quarterly estimated tax payments for 2025 if you had little withholding to cover SE tax this year.

If you want, I can:
- Run a precise, line‑by‑line estimate for federal tax, SE tax, and estimated payment amounts using both home‑office methods. I’ll need confirmation on the clarifying items above (especially whether the student loan interest was paid in 2024 and whether the $650 are self‑employment expenses).
- Or draft an email to a CPA with the facts and ask them to prepare the return / advise further.

