--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 8187 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. I’ll summarize what is (and isn’t) deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, what federal (and a few CA) forms you’ll need, and next steps/questions if you want me to run exact numbers.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (because your only clear itemizable amount you listed is $2,000 of charitable gifts, which is well below the single standard deduction).
- Your contracting income should be reported on Schedule C; you can take a home-office deduction for the 100 sq ft exclusive office. For ease with your $15,000 contractor income, the simplified home-office method ($5/sqft = $500) is often reasonable; the actual method (allocating ~14.5% of rent/utilities/etc.) could give a larger deduction but requires more paperwork.
- Student loan interest has an annual limit ($2,500) and phases out at higher MAGI. With your rough gross income (~$101k) you are likely above the phaseout and may not be eligible for that deduction — but adjustments (half of self‑employment tax, retirement contributions, etc.) could change MAGI, so I can check it precisely if you want.
- Unreimbursed “employee” expenses are not deductible for W-2 employees under current law; if the $650 are business expenses for your contracting work, then they’re deductible on Schedule C.

Details and how each item is treated

1) Income to report
- W-2 from TechStart: report on Form 1040 (W-2 wages).
- Contracting $15,000: report on Schedule C (self-employment/business income). This income is subject to income tax and self-employment tax.
- Interest/dividends: $920 (savings) and $195 (Schwab) are taxable interest/dividends and reported on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 if necessary).

2) Home office
- Requirement: must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft (limit 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft = $500 deduction. Easy — report on Schedule C (line for home office) — no Form 8829 needed.
  b) Actual-expense method: you allocate a business-use percentage = (100 / 690 ≈ 14.5%). Then multiply that % by rent, utilities, homeowners/renter insurance (and allowable depreciation if you owned). Example for rent: $2,400/mo = $28,800/yr → 14.5% ≈ $4,179 of rent allocable to the home office (plus part of utilities). If you use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 (if you’re self-employed) and attach it to Schedule C.
- Practical note: because your contracting income is modest ($15k), the simplified method is often simpler and still offers a meaningful deduction. Also, home-office deduction cannot create a net business loss that offsets other income (there are rules about limitations and carryovers), so the full actual amount may be limited if your business has little or no net profit after other business expenses.

3) Student loan interest ($3,600 paid)
- The maximum above-the-line deduction in recent years has been $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). This deduction is taken on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income).
- There is a MAGI phaseout for single filers; with gross income in the ~\$100k range you are likely above the phaseout and may not get this deduction. However, MAGI is computed after certain adjustments (including half of self‑employment tax and some retirement contributions), so it’s worth running the numbers precisely to confirm eligibility.

4) Charitable contributions ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). With only $2,000 of charitable donations and no other large deductible items provided (no mortgage interest or significant property taxes shown), itemizing will almost certainly be lower than the standard deduction for a single filer.
- If you itemize, keep receipts. For cash gifts > $250 you need written acknowledgement from the charity.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job: not deductible under current federal law (suspended through 2025 by TCJA).
- If they are business expenses related to your contracting work: deductible on Schedule C (ordinary and necessary business expenses).

6) Self-employment tax and QBI
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net self-employment earnings. You calculate it on Schedule SE. You may deduct one half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) equal to up to 20% of qualified business income from the contracting activity, subject to limits—this can reduce taxable income. With your numbers it may provide a meaningful additional deduction; whether and how much depends on taxable income calculations after adjustments.

7) Estimated taxes / withholdings
