Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Below is a clear, practical rundown of what’s deductible for your 2024 return, whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction, how the home‑office works, and the federal (and short California) forms you’ll likely need. I include calculations using the numbers you provided and action items.

Quick summary
- You will most likely take the standard deduction (your itemized deductible amounts look smaller than the standard deduction for single filers).  
- Claim your contracting income and related expenses on Schedule C (and Schedule SE for self‑employment tax). The home‑office deduction is available because you have self‑employment income and an exclusive 100 sq ft workspace.  
- The $3,600 student‑loan interest is probably not fully deductible because the student‑loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out for higher MAGI — with your income it will likely be reduced or disallowed. Check your exact MAGI.  
- The $2,000 cash gift to Northwest Harvest is an itemized charitable contribution; it only helps if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

1) Which deduction method: standard vs itemize
- Itemized deductions you listed: $2,000 (charitable). (You didn’t list mortgage interest, state/local taxes, or large medical expenses.)  
- Standard deduction (single) for 2024: $14,600. (Confirm on IRS website or your tax software when you prepare return.)  
=> With only $2,000 of itemized deductions, you should take the standard deduction.

2) Student‑loan interest ($3,600)
- The student‑loan interest deduction (Form 1040, Schedule 1 adjustment) max is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). Phaseout range for single filers has historically started around $75k and ended around $90k (confirm exact 2024 thresholds).  
- Your gross income estimate: W‑2 $84,900 + contracting $15,000 + interest $1,115 = ~ $101,015 (before adjustments). That likely puts you above the phaseout end, so you probably cannot claim this deduction.  
- Action: get Form 1098‑E from MOHELA and calculate MAGI precisely; tax software/CPA will apply the phaseout.

3) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize. Since standard deduction is likely larger, this probably won’t reduce your federal tax for 2024. Still keep the receipt in case circumstances change (or for state tax considerations).

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job: not deductible on your federal return (suspended for most employees by TCJA for tax years 2018–2025).  
- If they are business expenses for your contracting work (self‑employment): deduct them on Schedule C (they reduce net self‑employment income and lower both income tax and self‑employment tax).

5) Home‑office deduction (you qualify if space is used regularly and exclusively for business)
You have 100 sq ft exclusive home office; home is 690 sq ft total.

Two methods:
A. Simplified method: $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829 required).
B. Regular/actual‑expense method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of business use.
- Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4927% (≈14.5%).  
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Home‑office portion of rent ≈ 14.4927% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176.  
- If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc., 14.5% of those may also be deductible as home office expense. Using actual expenses will likely give you a much larger deduction than the simplified method given your rent, but actual method requires Form 8829 (and careful records). Also the home‑office deduction cannot exceed the net profit from that business (special rules apply if it creates a business loss). The deduction for a self‑employed taxpayer reduces both income tax and the net subject to self‑employment tax.

Recommendation: Because your rent is high, the actual‑expense method will probably provide a larger deduction (example above shows ~$4,176 of rent alone). Use actual method if you can document rent/utilities; otherwise simplified is simpler but smaller.

6) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You must report the $15,000 contracting income (and any business expenses) on Schedule C. Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).  
- Self‑employment tax rate approx. 15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings; half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.  
- If net self‑employment income remains positive, you may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) — QBI rules are complex and depend on taxable income and type of services; check with software or a tax professional.

7) Interest income
- $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 total interest. Report on Form 1040 (interest). You do not need Schedule B unless taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual return.  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (student loan interest if eligible, half of SE tax as an adjustment is calculated elsewhere but reported here).  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $15,000 contracting income and related expenses (including home‑office deduction if using simplified method you put the deduction on Schedule C; if using actual expenses, complete Form 8829 and transfer amounts to Schedule C).  
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you choose the actual expense method). Not used with the simplified method.  
- Form 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC: if your clients provided 1099s, include them; if they didn’t, you still must report the income.  
- State: California Form 540 (CA state return) — state tax rules differ in details, but CA generally requires reporting the same federal taxable income as a starting point (verify home office conformity with CA rules or consult CA guidance).

9) Documentation to keep
- W‑2 from TechStart Inc.  
- Records of contracting income (bank deposits, 1099‑NECs, invoices).  
- Receipts for business expenses (the $650), and details of any other business costs.  
- Rent receipts/lease, utility bills, renter’s insurance to support actual home‑office allocation.  
- 1098‑E from MOHELA for student‑loan interest.  
- Charity receipt for Northwest Harvest (written acknowledgement for cash gifts over $250).  
- 1099‑INTs for interest income (Chase, Schwab).

10) Practical next steps
- Decide actual vs simplified home‑office method. Based on rent, actual method probably gives a larger deduction — gather rent and utility bills and use Form 8829.  
- If your $650 unreimbursed expenses were for contracting, categorize and record them for Schedule C. If they were W‑2 employee expenses, they are not deductible on your 2024 federal return.  
- Get Form 1098‑E from MOHELA and let your tax software or preparer compute whether any student loan interest deduction survives the MAGI phaseout.  
- Use tax software or a CPA to prepare 1040 + Schedules C/SE (the software will also apply QBI rules and compute whether itemizing ever helps).  
- Consider estimated tax payments next year if you’ll have similar self‑employment income (to avoid underpayment penalties).

Caveats / suggestions
- I used approximate thresholds and common IRS rules (student‑loan interest phaseout, simplified vs actual home‑office rules). Confirm exact 2024 phaseout thresholds and standard deduction with up‑to‑date IRS guidance or tax software.  
- QBI and home‑office interactions can be nuanced; if you want me to estimate likely tax owed or run a sample calculation with different home‑office choices (actual vs simplified), I can run rough numbers for you.

If you’d like, I can:
- Walk through a rough tax calculation showing taxable income, estimated federal income tax, and self‑employment tax using the actual vs simplified home‑office method; or
- Draft a checklist/email you can send to a CPA with all needed documents; or
- Prepare a form list and fill in exact numbers if you provide any other expenses (utilities, internet, business supplies, 1099s, etc.).

Which of those would you like next?

