I can walk through this and give practical next steps. I’m not a CPA — this is general tax guidance. A few quick clarifying questions before I calculate precise numbers or give a final recommendation:
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your IT contracting work (self-employment) or to your W-2 job at TechStart?
- Do you rent (paying $2,400/month) or own the place?
- Were the student-loan interest payments made in 2024, and did you receive a Form 1098-E from MOHELA for 2024? (You mentioned 2016–2019 — if those payments were made in earlier years, they aren’t deductible on your 2024 return.)

Below is a clear, practical breakdown of how each item is treated, what forms you’ll use, and whether itemizing likely makes sense.

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: TechStart Inc $84,900 — reported on Form 1040 using your W-2.
- Self-employment/contracting: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). All 3 clients’ payments count as business income whether or not you get 1099-NEC.
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) and $195 (Charles Schwab) — report as taxable interest (1099‑INT/1099 from brokerage).

2) Home office
- Only available if you are self-employed (independent contractor). W-2 employees generally cannot claim a home office deduction.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft => 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual-expense) method: allocate the business-use percentage of actual home expenses. Your business-use % = 100 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%. If you pay $2,400/mo = $28,800/yr rent, the home-office share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,179. (You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., which would increase the deduction.) Use Form 8829 (or report on Schedule C depending on method) for the computation.
- Recommendation: for rent-only numbers you gave, the regular method (≈$4,179) likely gives a larger deduction than the simplified $500. This deduction reduces self-employment net income on Schedule C.

3) Self-employment tax and estimated taxes
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self-employment tax (~15.3% on net SE income), reported on Schedule SE. You’ll also be able to deduct half the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- If you expect to owe tax because of the contracting income, consider making estimated quarterly payments to avoid penalties.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600 reported)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is limited (historically up to $2,500 per year) and is an above-the-line adjustment on Form 1040 (you’d enter it on Schedule 1 as an adjustment).
- The deduction phases out at higher modified AGI levels for single filers. Based on the income numbers you gave (W-2 $84,900 + $15,000 contracting + interest ≈ $101k gross before expenses), you may be above the phase-out range and therefore may not be able to claim the deduction — but this depends on your exact MAGI calculation and the tax-year 2024 phaseout thresholds. Business expenses (including the home office share) lower your AGI, which could affect eligibility.
- Important: only interest paid in the tax year (and reported on Form 1098‑E) is deductible for that tax year. If the $3,600 came from earlier years (2016–2019), it isn’t deductible on 2024 tax return.

5) Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest)
- If you itemize on Schedule A, you can deduct cash gifts to qualified charities; keep your receipt.
- If you take the standard deduction, you generally cannot deduct these donations on your federal return (there were temporary exceptions in prior years, but those are not generally in effect for 2024).
- California state rules differ somewhat; check state treatment.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($650)
- If these are expenses tied to your W-2 job (unreimbursed employee expenses), they are not deductible for federal income tax under current law (suspended through 2025 for most employees).
- If they are business expenses related to your contracting work, deduct them on Schedule C.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- The standard deduction for single filers is almost always larger than a small set of itemized deductions. With only $2,000 of charitable contributions listed (and no mortgage interest, significant state/local taxes, large medical expenses, etc., provided so far), you will most likely be better off taking the standard deduction on your federal return.
- Note: home office and other self-employment business expenses do NOT go on Schedule A — they reduce taxable income via Schedule C regardless of whether you itemize.
- If you want, I can run a quick comparison if you tell me any additional deductible items (state income/property taxes paid, mortgage interest, large medical costs, etc.).

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest if eligible)
- Schedule C — self-employment income and business expenses (contracting $15,000, home office share, $650 contractor expenses if applicable)
- Schedule SE — self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method for home office)
- Schedule A — itemized deductions (only if you choose to itemize)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if received from clients) — report regardless
- 1099‑INT / brokerage statements for interest/dividends
- 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you received one)
- California Form 540 (state return) — state rules differ; student loan interest and other items may be treated differently on your CA return.

9) Practical next steps I recommend
- Confirm whether the $650 expenses are contractor-related and whether you rent or own.
- Locate Form 1098‑E for 2024 from MOHELA (if you paid interest in 2024) — if you didn’t receive one and payments were not made in 2024, that interest won’t be deductible for 2024.
- Decide whether to use simplified or regular home-office method; if you have rent and a sizable home-office share, regular method likely gives a bigger deduction — collect rent and utility statements.
- If you want, I can run a quick rough tax estimate (federal) using either home-office method and show: expected taxable income, estimated self-employment tax, and whether student loan interest would be allowed given the resulting AGI.

If you want me to produce that rough estimate, please answer the clarifying questions (especially whether the $650 is contractor-related and whether you rent or own) and confirm whether the $3,600 of student-loan interest was paid in 2024 and whether you received a 1098‑E for 2024.

