Summary answers up front
- Most likely: take the federal standard deduction (single) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state/local tax, mortgage interest, big medical expenses) you didn’t list.
- Your self‑employment income (the $15,000 contracting) is reported on Schedule C; business expenses (including the home‑office share of rent and the $650 unreimbursed expenses if those are business related) reduce that profit.
- Home office: you can deduct either the simplified method ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft) or the actual-expense method. With 100 sq ft and rent $2,400/month, the actual method will generally be larger — see the example below.
- Student loan interest: you can only deduct interest actually paid in tax year 2024 (up to $2,500, subject to income limits). I need to confirm whether the $3,600 figure is interest paid in 2024 or historic payments.
- Interest from Chase/Schwab is taxable as ordinary interest and reported on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total interest > $1,500).
- Key federal forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (self‑employment business income), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (business use of home — if using actual expense method), and possibly Form 8995/8995-A if you claim the QBI (20%) deduction. For state, file California Form 540 (state rules may differ).
- Consider estimated tax payments if you expect similar self‑employment income next year.

