Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can walk through what’s allowed, give an estimate of how the home office affects your tax picture, and tell you which forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag uncertainties and what I need if you want me to run exact numbers.

Quick assumptions I’ll use below (please correct if any are wrong):
- You’re single, California resident, no dependents.
- W-2 wages = $84,900 (no pre-tax 401(k) or HSA amounts provided).
- Self‑employment (contracting) gross = $15,000; the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses related to that contracting work (if they were unreimbursed employee expenses from W‑2 work, they generally are NOT deductible).
- Interest income = $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115.
- Rent = $2,400/month = $28,800/year for a 690 sq ft apartment; home office = 100 sq ft exclusive and regular use for business.

1) Deductions you can (likely) take
- Business expenses for your contracting (Schedule C): the $650 (if for the contracting) and any other ordinary & necessary expenses (supplies, phone, advertising, software, business mileage, etc.). These reduce your self‑employment net income.
- Home office deduction (if your contracting is run from home and the 100 sq ft area is used exclusively and regularly as your principal place of business). See more below on methods.
- One-half of self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income.
- Interest income is taxable and reported on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Charitable gifts ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest) — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you generally cannot deduct these.
- Student loan interest ($3,600 paid) — maximum allowed deduction is $2,500, but it phases out for higher MAGI. Whether you can actually deduct any of it depends on your modified AGI (see below).
- State and local taxes (SALT) and mortgage interest — you didn’t list these; SALT is deductible on Schedule A but capped at $10,000 if you itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible for most taxpayers under current rules.

2) Home office: how to treat it (two methods)
You qualify for the deduction only if the space is used exclusively and regularly for your contracting business and is your principal place of business.

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
  - For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction.
  - Simpler to compute and no Form 8829 depreciation calculation.
- Regular method:
  - Allocate actual home expenses to business by the business‑use percentage (100 sq ft / 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%).
  - You can deduct the business share of rent, utilities, renters’ insurance, etc. (if you owned the home you could also deduct depreciation, property tax portion, etc.).
  - With your numbers: annual rent = $28,800; business share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,178. You can also add allocable utilities if you pay them.
  - This is usually larger than the simplified amount for your situation, but it requires more recordkeeping and Form 8829.

Practical note: The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit, which lowers both income tax and self‑employment tax. The deduction cannot exceed your net business income (before the home‑office expense) for that business.

3) Student loan interest deduction — likely limited or disallowed
- The student loan interest deduction has a $2,500 maximum, but it phases out for higher modified AGI. For single filers the phaseout (recent years) has been roughly $75k–$90k MAGI; with your W‑2 + interest + contracting income you’re likely near or above the phaseout range.
- Example (approximate):
  - If you use the regular home office allocation (≈ $4,178) and deduct business expenses, your net self‑employment income will fall, but in quick estimates your MAGI still looks likely to be at or above the phaseout range — meaning you may not get the student loan interest deduction. If you want, I can calculate a more precise MAGI estimate with any missing numbers (withholding, retirement deferrals, other deductions).

4) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Standard deduction (single) is almost always larger unless you have significant deductible items: large mortgage interest, large state & local taxes (SALT up to $10k limit), medical expenses above the threshold, or sizable charitable gifts.
- From what you’ve listed:
  - Charitable = $2,000.
  - No mortgage interest shown.
  - SALT not provided (state income tax paid could help but capped at $10,000).
  - Therefore, it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total.
- Recommendation: Take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (large state tax payments, mortgage interest, unreimbursed medical > threshold, etc.). I can compare with exact numbers if you provide withheld state income tax or property tax paid.

5) Forms you’ll need / where things are reported
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — report interest income and certain adjustments (student loan interest adjustment if allowed).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your contracting income and business expenses (including home office deduction amount). If you use the regular home office method, the home office deduction is computed on Form 8829 and flows to Schedule C.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if using the regular method). If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on your net self‑employment income.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (report charitable gift of $2,000, state & local taxes, etc.).
- Forms 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC — you should receive these from clients if applicable; report all self‑employment income even if you didn’t receive a 1099.
- Form 1098‑E — you should receive this from your loan servicer showing student loan interest paid; keep for records.
- California state return — you’ll also file CA Form 540; some deductions differ on state return.

6) Other items to consider
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income and will owe self‑employment tax, you should consider making estimated quarterly tax payments (Form 1040-ES) unless sufficient tax is withheld from your W‑2 to cover the tax due. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when filing, estimated payments are usually required.
- Retirement contributions: You may be able to reduce taxable income by contributing to a SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k) or traditional IRA (subject to limits and rules) — these can lower AGI and possibly preserve the student loan interest deduction if that’s borderline.
- Keep good records: receipts for charitable gifts, business expenses, home office allocation basis (floor plan, how you computed square footage), 1099s, and the 1098‑E record for student loan interest.

7) Rough numeric example (illustrative)
- Using assumptions above and regular home office allocation (approximate):
  - Contracting gross: $15,000
  - Business expenses: $650 + home office rent alloc ≈ $4,178 → net business ≈ $10,172
  - Self‑employment tax (approx): ~$1,438 → half deductible ≈ $719
  - Total income before adjustments: 84,900 + 1,115 + 10,172 ≈ $96,187
  - Subtract half SE ≈ $719 will reduce AGI to ≈ $95,468 (before any student loan interest deduction).
  - With MAGI around $95k, the student loan interest deduction (which phases out around $75k–$90k) is likely limited or disallowed.
  - Itemized deductions from what you listed (charity $2,000) are well below the standard deduction, so standard deduction is likely better.

If you want precise numbers
- Tell me:
  - Whether the $650 expenses are for the contracting (if not, tell me what they were for).
  - Any pre-tax contributions on your W‑2 (401(k), HSA, FSA amounts).
  - Any state tax paid or withheld last year (or property tax).
  - Whether you received any 1099s and whether the contracting income was already net of expenses.
  - Whether you want me to use the simplified or regular home office method (I can show both).
I can then run exact estimates, prepare a checklist of forms filled with line amounts, or draft notes you can use to prepare or give to a tax preparer.

