- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if using the regular method). If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on your net self‑employment income.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (report charitable gift of $2,000, state & local taxes, etc.).
- Forms 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC — you should receive these from clients if applicable; report all self‑employment income even if you didn’t receive a 1099.
- Form 1098‑E — you should receive this from your loan servicer showing student loan interest paid; keep for records.
- California state return — you’ll also file CA Form 540; some deductions differ on state return.

6) Other items to consider
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income and will owe self‑employment tax, you should consider making estimated quarterly tax payments (Form 1040-ES) unless sufficient tax is withheld from your W‑2 to cover the tax due. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when filing, estimated payments are usually required.
- Retirement contributions: You may be able to reduce taxable income by contributing to a SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k) or traditional IRA (subject to limits and rules) — these can lower AGI and possibly preserve the student loan interest deduction if that’s borderline.
- Keep good records: receipts for charitable gifts, business expenses, home office allocation basis (floor plan, how you computed square footage), 1099s, and the 1098‑E record for student loan interest.

