Thanks — this is a good set of numbers. Below I’ll cover (1) what’s deductible, (2) whether to itemize vs take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office (with quick numbers comparing simplified vs actual), and (4) which federal and California forms you’ll typically need — plus a short checklist of next steps and clarifying questions.

Summary recommendation (quick)
- For federal taxes you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing, given only $2,000 of charitable gifts and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes listed.
- Because you have self-employment (IT contracting) you can and should claim business expenses (including a home-office deduction) for that business on Schedule C. That will reduce your federal taxable income and self-employment tax.
- Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” deduction (up to $2,500) but it phases out depending on your MAGI. With your income as provided, you are likely above the phaseout and probably cannot claim it — I show the numbers below so you can confirm.

1) What you can (and cannot) deduct — federal perspective
- Deductible (allowed):
  - Business expenses for your contracting work (Schedule C): e.g., home office allocation for the contracting activity, supplies, software, mileage (if applicable), phone/internet portion used for business, equipment, etc.
  - Half of self-employment tax (an above-the-line adjustment).
  - Interest on student loans (up to $2,500) — only if your MAGI is below the IRS phaseout for the year. You should have a Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the year.
  - Charitable contributions only if you itemize (see itemize vs standard below). Northwest Harvest is typically a tax-exempt charity (keep the receipt).

- Not deductible (federally) given current law:
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $650) — miscellaneous itemized employee expenses are suspended through 2025 for most employees, so these are not deductible if they are costs tied to your W‑2 job.
  - Charitable contributions are not deductible if you take the standard deduction (no general above-the-line charitable deduction in most recent tax years).

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- Standard deduction for a single filer is much larger than $2,000 in recent years. Even without quoting an exact inflation-adjusted number, your itemizable amounts you listed are small:
  - Charitable: $2,000
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses: $0 (not deductible)
  - No mortgage interest or significant state/local tax listed (SALT cap also applies)
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction on your federal return unless you have other itemizable amounts (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses above threshold, or additional charitable gifts) not listed here.

California (state)
- You still file CA Form 540. California itemized deduction rules differ in some respects from federal — but if you don’t itemize for federal you will likely not have enough to itemize in CA either, given only $2,000 in charitable gifts. Business income and home office deductions for your self-employment generally flow through to California, but there are some CA-specific adjustments — consult CA instructions or a tax pro if state-specific details matter.

3) Home-office treatment (important for your contracting income)
- Requirements: The IRS requires exclusive and regular use of a part of your home for business and that it be your principal place of business for that activity. You said the 100 sq ft is exclusive — that supports eligibility for the contracting business portion. If that space is used for both your W‑2 job and contracting, you can only deduct the portion that’s for the self‑employment business.
- Two methods to compute:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot (up to 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) based on business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
    - Your rent = $2,400/month = $28,800/year.
    - Home-office share of rent = 14.4928% * $28,800 ≈ $4,175 (plus a proportional share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have those).
- Quick comparison using your numbers:
  - If you use the simplified method: home-office deduction = $500.
    - Net self-employment income = $15,000 - $500 = $14,500.
    - Estimated self-employment tax on that (approx): ~ $2,000 (this is an estimate); half of that is deductible.
  - If you use actual method (and you allocate rent only): home-office deduction ≈ $4,175.
    - Net self-employment income = $15,000 - $4,175 = $10,825.
    - Estimated self-employment tax on that (approx): ~ $1,530; half deductible.
  - So with the rent numbers you gave, the actual-method home-office deduction looks substantially better than the simplified-method amount — provided you legitimately use the office exclusively for your contracting. Also include your share of utilities/internet if used for the business (that increases the actual-method benefit).
- How to report:
  - The self-employment income and business expenses (including home office) are reported on Schedule C.
  - If you use the actual method for home office, you normally fill out Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it with Schedule C. The simplified method is computed separately and reported on Schedule C as well.

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 as an above-the-line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040). However it phases out at higher MAGI levels. With the numbers you gave (W‑2 $84,900 + contracting $15,000 + ~$1,115 interest = total gross ~ $101,015 before business deductions), once you account for self-employment income and adjustments, you are likely above the phaseout for a single filer and therefore unlikely to be able to claim the $3,600 you listed. You should check the Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for interest paid in 2024 and confirm MAGI phaseout ranges for 2024; if you are under the phaseout you can claim up to $2,500 (not the full $3,600).

5) Forms you’ll typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments such as student loan interest or other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report contracting income ($15,000) and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual-expense method for home-office deduction.
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (keeps support if you claim it).
- Form W‑2 (from TechStart Inc.) and any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients (if issued).
- Form 1040‑ES — for paying quarterly estimated taxes (if you expect to owe estimated tax because you have self-employment income).

California forms
- Form 540 (California Resident Income Tax Return) and CA schedules as applicable. Business income reported on federal normally flows to California with certain adjustments.

6) Other points and recommendations
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable donation, all 1099s, Form 1098‑E, rent/utility statements, logs for business use of home, records of any other business expenses (equipment, supplies, software, mileage).
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax payments. Use Form 1040‑ES to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If you haven’t already: check whether each client will send you a 1099‑NEC. Regardless, you must report all self-employment income.
- Consider retirement savings: contributing to a SEP IRA, Solo 401(k), or traditional IRA could reduce taxable income (SEP/solo options reduce AGI for the business owner and also reduce self-employment tax base potentially). If you’re interested I can run examples.
- If you want a precise tax estimate I can run the numbers (federal tax, self-employment tax, and approximate refund/amount due) using the choices (simplified vs actual home-office; with/without student loan deduction). Tell me whether the home office is used exclusively for contracting (and if you have any other business expenses) and whether you want me to assume the actual method and include utilities.

7) Quick checklist of what I need from you if you want a calculated estimate
- Confirm: Is the 100 sq ft used exclusively for the contracting work (not for your W‑2 job)?
- Any additional business expenses (equipment, software subscriptions, phone/internet % used for business, mileage, business meals, etc.)?
- Do you have a Form 1098‑E for student loan interest paid in 2024? (if yes, how much interest was paid in 2024)
- Any mortgage interest, property tax, or other large deductible expenses not mentioned?
- Do you want me to estimate your federal tax owed/refund and self-employment tax under both simplified and actual home-office methods?

If you want, I can run a side-by-side estimated tax calculation now (federal only) using the actual home-office allocation vs the simplified method and show estimated AGI, taxable income, and approximate tax & self-employment tax amounts. Would you like me to do that?

