Thanks — that’s enough to give a clear, practical picture. I’ll summarize what you can and can’t deduct, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, what forms you’ll need, and give a rough worked example so you can see the tax effects. A couple of quick questions are at the end (they’ll change the result).

Summary (short)
- You should report the $15,000 contracting income on Schedule C (self‑employment). That makes you self‑employed for that income.
- Home office deduction: Allowed for your contracting work if the 100 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for that business. You can use the regular method (your share of rent, utilities, etc.) or the simplified method ($5 × sq ft). Regular method looks better with the rent you showed.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are NOT deductible for most taxpayers under current law. If the $650 were expenses for your contracting business, they ARE deductible on Schedule C.
- Charitable gift ($2,000): deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If your itemizable deductions are less than the standard deduction, you cannot deduct that $2,000.
- Student loan interest: the maximum above‑the‑line deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). Whether you can use it (and how much) depends on your modified AGI for 2024 and whether the $3,600 you listed refers to interest paid in 2024 (only interest paid in the tax year is deductible).
- You’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction unless your total itemizable deductions (charity + state & local tax + mortgage interest + large medical expenses) exceed the standard deduction. From the items you listed, itemizing is unlikely to beat the standard deduction.

Key forms you will use
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for some adjustments like student loan interest (if allowed)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (home office, the $650 if it’s a business expense, etc.)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax on your contracting profit
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the regular method for home office; if you use the simplified method, you record the simplified home office deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (charitable donation would go here)
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you receive them from clients) — include them on Schedule C
- 1099‑INTs (Chase / Schwab statements) — include the interest on Form 1040
- (Possibly) Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction on qualified business income from Schedule C; it’s worth checking)

How to treat the home office (practical)
- Eligibility: must be used regularly and exclusively for your contracting business, and be either your principal place of business or a place to meet clients, etc.
- Measurement: you gave 100 sq ft exclusive use out of 690 sq ft total → business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Regular method (likely better given your rent): allocate 14.49% of deductible home expenses (rent, a portion of utilities, renters insurance, repairs related to the office) to business. Use Form 8829 to calculate.
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829).
- Limitation: home office deduction can’t create a loss from the business in some cases — excess (if any) may be suspended and carried forward. Also the deduction reduces net profit, which reduces self‑employment tax.

Rough worked example (based on your numbers and a few assumptions)
Numbers you provided:
- W‑2 wages: $84,900
- Self‑employment (contracting) income: $15,000
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115
- Rent: $2,400/mo = $28,800/year
- Home office: 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 14.4928% → home office portion of rent = $28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,176
- Business unreimbursed expenses (assume these are for contracting): $650
