Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Below is a practical, step‑by‑step summary of what you can likely deduct, whether itemizing will help, how to treat the home office, and which tax forms you’ll need. I’ll also call out a few places I need clarification on to be precise.

1) Summary of the items you listed
- W-2 from TechStart Inc: $84,900 — reported on Form 1040 (w/ W-2).
- Self‑employment (IT contracting): $15,000 from 3 clients — report on Schedule C (profit/loss). Expect 1099‑NEC forms from clients (or you still report it even if you didn’t get 1099s).
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (and on Schedule B only if over the Schedule B threshold).
- Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive use; total home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month.
- Student loan interest: you listed $3,600 (MOHELA) — need clarification (see below).
- Charity: $2,000 to Northwest Harvest (you have receipt).
- $650 unreimbursed expenses — need clarification whether these are business expenses (for your contracting) or unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job.

2) Which deductions apply and key limits
- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 per year, subject to income (modified AGI) phaseouts. If the $3,600 is interest you actually paid in tax year 2024, you can claim up to $2,500 (not the full $3,600). You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing the interest paid in 2024. If the $3,600 refers to prior years’ interest, only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return.
- Charitable gift ($2,000):
  - Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it on 1040 (aside from any temporary special rules that might have applied in certain years — check current guidance). Keep your receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650):
  - If these are business (self-employed) expenses, deduct them on Schedule C.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on federal returns for tax years 2018–2025 under current law (TCJA).
- Interest income ($1,115):
  - Taxable on Form 1040. If total interest > the Schedule B threshold (typically $1,500) or you have certain foreign accounts, you’d complete Schedule B. At $1,115 you’re under that threshold, so no Schedule B required (but still report it).
- Self‑employment tax:
  - You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings; that’s computed on Schedule SE. You may deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- State tax / SALT cap:
  - If you paid California state income tax or property taxes, those go on Schedule A up to the $10,000 SALT cap if you itemize. You didn’t list these, so I assume none; if you have them, include them when comparing itemize vs standard.

3) Home office — two methods and recommended choice
You can choose either the simplified method or the regular method for a home office deduction. The home office deduction applies only to self‑employment income (Schedule C).

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft: 100 * $5 = $500.
  - No Form 8829; simpler recordkeeping.
- Regular method (allocates actual expenses):
  - Business use percentage = home office sq ft / total home sq ft = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Allocated rent portion = $28,800 * 14.49% ≈ $4,176.
  - You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (the business portion), etc., using the same percentage. You’d report this on Form 8829 (if you’re claiming actual expenses for a self‑employed Schedule C).
- Recommendation based on your numbers:
  - Regular method gives ≈ $4,176 just from rent, which is much larger than the $500 simplified amount. So regular method will likely yield a bigger deduction. Use regular method if you have good records and the office use is exclusive and regular.

Important home office rules:
- The space must be used regularly and exclusively for the business (no dual personal use).
- The deduction cannot exceed net business income from that activity (suspension rules apply if it creates a loss).
- Keep documentation: floor plan, photos, lease/rent statements, utility bills, etc.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For tax year 2024 (single), the standard deduction is likely larger than your listed itemizable expenses.
  - Itemizable items you listed: charity $2,000 + (possibly state taxes/property taxes—but you didn’t list any) + mortgage interest (none listed) = ≈ $2,000.
  - Unless you have additional itemizable deductions (significant state tax or property tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), itemizing with only $2,000 in charitable gifts will be far less than the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: You will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you also have significant SALT or mortgage interest or other itemizable amounts that push total itemized deductions above the standard deduction.

5) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments like student loan interest deduction and the deduction for half of self‑employment tax.
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report self‑employment income and business expenses (your contracting).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the home office (allocate rent and other home expenses). Not required for the simplified method.
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (use to support the student loan interest deduction).
- Form 1099‑NEC (or 1099‑MISC) — from clients reporting nonemployee compensation if provided; you report income even if no 1099.
- Form 1099‑INT — bank/broker interest (Chase, Schwab).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (to claim the $2,000 charity and any other itemized deductions).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend totals exceed the threshold (generally $1,500) or you have certain foreign accounts — your interest total is $1,115, so Schedule B likely not required.
- California state return: CA Form 540 (and associated schedules). State rules differ from federal (including student loan interest and other items) so check CA specifics.

6) Records to keep
- Receipts for charitable gift ($2,000 receipt).
- Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.
- Proof of interest income (1099‑INT).
- Records for self‑employment income (invoices, 1099s) and receipts for expenses (the $650 and anything else).
- Documentation for home office exclusive use: floor plan, photos, rental receipts, utility bills, etc.

7) A few cautions and action items / clarifying questions
- Clarify the student loan interest: Is the $3,600 interest the amount you actually paid in 2024? If yes, you can likely claim up to $2,500 (subject to AGI phaseout). If not, what was actually paid in 2024?
- Clarify the $650 unreimbursed expenses: are those business expenses for the contracting work (then deductible on Schedule C) or unreimbursed expenses as an employee (not deductible federally)?
- Do you have any other deductible items not listed (property tax, state income tax paid, mortgage interest, high medical expenses, etc.)?
- Do you want me to run an estimated tax calculation (federal + self‑employment tax) using these numbers so you can see approximate tax owed or refund? I can do a rough estimate quickly.

8) Quick rough example (to illustrate how the pieces interact)
Assumptions:
- Use regular home office method (rent allocation ≈ $4,176).
- $650 are business expenses.
- Student loan interest deductible limited to $2,500 (assuming you qualify by AGI).
Then:
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 15,000 − (4,176 + 650) = 10,174.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 10,174 * 0.9235 * 15.3% ≈ ~$1,430 (approx). Half of that (~$715) is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Your AGI will include W‑2 wages + net SE income + interest, less above‑the‑line adjustments (half SE tax and student loan interest). That AGI is what you compare to the standard deduction to get taxable income.
This is a rough sketch; I can produce precise numbers if you want.

9) Next steps I can do for you
- Clarify the student loan year/payment and the unreimbursed expense details.
- Run a precise tax estimate (federal + self‑employment tax) using either simplified or regular home office methods so you can see which is better.
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing or for a tax preparer.

If you want, tell me:
1) Was the $3,600 interest paid in 2024 (and do you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA)? 
2) Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the IT contracting work (or were they for your W‑2 job)?
3) Any state income tax or property tax paid in 2024, or mortgage interest paid?

I’ll then run numbers and show a side‑by‑side comparison (standard deduction vs itemizing and simplified vs regular home office method).

