Length: 9260 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a practical, organized rundown for your 2024 federal return based on the facts you gave, plus a few clarifying questions and recommended next steps. I’m not a CPA — this is general information to help you prepare; for binding tax advice or to handle complex issues I recommend a tax professional.

Quick facts you gave (used below)
- Filing status: Single (San Diego, CA)
- W-2 wages from TechStart Inc: $84,900
- Independent contracting income (3 clients): $15,000
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115
- Home: renting 690 sq ft; home office = 100 sq ft exclusive use
- Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year
- Charitable cash gifts: $2,000 (you have receipt)
- Student loan interest: you wrote $3,600 (MOHELA, 2016–2019) — please confirm whether any of this interest was paid in 2024; only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible
- $650 “unreimbursed expenses” — please confirm whether these are employee unreimbursed expenses (not deductible for most taxpayers on federal return), or business expenses related to your contracting (deductible on Schedule C)

What you can deduct (federal)
- W-2: report on Form 1040 (Box 1). Any federal/state tax withheld is reported on 1040.
- Contracting income: report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (this includes supplies, software, phone proportion, business portion of internet, and the $650 if those are business expenses).
- Home office (self-employed): deductible on Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 x 100 sq ft = $500 (no Form 8829 required).
  - Regular (actual-cost) method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Example using rent only: 14.49% × $28,800 rent ≈ $4,174. If you also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc., the regular method will typically yield a larger deduction than the simplified method in your case. Regular method uses Form 8829 (if you’re self-employed) to compute allowable deduction.
  - Note: home-office deduction requires exclusive and regular business use and that the space be your principal place of business or used for meeting clients, etc. Also the home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business (there are carryover rules for disallowed amounts).
- Self-employment tax: you must pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). Half of the SE tax is an “above-the-line” deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1, but it is limited by income (phaseouts) and only for interest paid in the tax year. You need Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024 to claim it. If your 2024 MAGI is above the phaseout range you may not get the full deduction (see IRS instructions or a tax pro for exact phaseout thresholds).
- Charitable cash donations: $2,000 — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Documentation (receipt) is required for cash contributions.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible on the federal return for most employees (suspended by TCJA through 2025). If the $650 is for your contracting (i.e., business expenses), deduct on Schedule C instead.

Standard deduction vs. itemize (federal)
- 2024 standard deduction for single: $14,600.
- Your likely itemizable deductions (that you listed) appear limited: $2,000 charitable + (do you have state/local taxes or mortgage interest? You didn’t list these). The SALT cap limits state and local taxes deduction to $10,000.
- Because your itemizable deductions appear much less than $14,600, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction on your federal return.
- Important nuance: the home-office deduction is NOT part of Schedule A itemizing — it reduces your business income on Schedule C (thus reduces AGI) whether or not you itemize. That makes your standard-deduction comparison easier: you can still take the home-office deduction and also take the standard deduction.

Example rough math (illustrative)
- Self-employed gross: $15,000
- If regular home-office rent portion ~ $4,174 and other business expenses $650 (if they are business), net business income ≈ $15,000 − $4,174 − $650 = $10,176.
- SE tax (approx): net × 92.35% × 15.3% ≈ $1,438; half of that (~$719) is deductible above-the-line.
- Total income before adjustments ≈ W-2 84,900 + net business 10,176 + interest 1,115 = ≈ $96,191.
- Subtract half SE tax ≈ 719 → AGI ≈ $95,472 (before any student loan interest adjustment).
- Subtract standard deduction $14,600 → taxable income ≈ $80,872 (very rough).
This is only illustrative to show magnitudes; final tax depends on exact allowable business expenses, actual student loan interest paid in 2024, any withholdings, credits, and precise tax-table/bracket calculations.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest, and the deductible half of SE tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to compute business use of home (home-office). If you use simplified method, you do not file Form 8829 but you still report the deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely you will not, based on given numbers)
- Schedule B — only if you have more than $1,500 of taxable interest (your total interest is $1,115, so likely not needed)
- Keep: Form W-2 from TechStart; any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC/1099-K for contracting (if you got one or more), 1099-INT from banks, 1098-E from MOHELA for student loan interest (if applicable), receipts for charitable contributions, records of business expenses, rent receipts, and a photograph/drawing of the home-office space and calculation if needed for substantiation.

California state filing
- You’ll file California Form 540 (or CA instructions for single filers). CA conforms to many federal rules but has differences — e.g., CA standard deduction is much lower than federal; check CA instructions for treatment of student loan interest and differences for business income deductions. If you paid state income taxes or property taxes, remember the federal SALT cap ($10,000) applies on federal Schedule A but state rules differ.

Recordkeeping you should keep
- Receipts for charitable donations
- 1098-E for student loan interest (for 2024)
- 1099s and W-2
- Business receipts, a mileage log if you use a vehicle, and documentation substantiating home-office square footage and expenses (rent invoices, utility bills, insurance, repairs)
- Proof of exclusivity/regularity of home-office use (photos, floor plan) if ever questioned

Key questions I need to give more precise answers
1. Is the $3,600 student loan interest amount money you paid in 2024, or were those payments in earlier years (2016–2019)? Only interest paid in 2024 can be claimed on the 2024 return.
2. Are the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses (i.e., expenses you incurred while working as a W-2 employee) or business expenses tied to your contracting work? If they’re business expenses, they belong on Schedule C; if they’re employee unreimbursed expenses, they are generally nondeductible for federal returns (through 2025).
3. Did you receive any 1099-NEC / 1099-K for the $15,000, or was there any federal/state tax withheld on that contracting income?
4. Do you own the home or rent? (You said pay $2,400/month — that suggests rent.)
5. Do you want me to run a more exact estimate of federal tax liability (approximate tax + self-employment tax) using the numbers above and any clarifications you provide?

