1) Home‑office deduction — how to treat it
- Eligible only if the workspace is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment/business and is your principal place of business (your 100 sq ft exclusive space sounds to meet that test for your contracting work).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × square footage (up to 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction.
  b) Regular (actual expenses) method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to the office. Your office is 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49% of the home.
     - Rent annual = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174 (this is the portion of rent allocable to the business). You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 for the regular method.
- Important: The home‑office deduction is only available against self‑employment (Schedule C) income. It’s not available for ordinary W‑2 employees (except very narrow exceptions).
- Practical note: The home‑office (actual) deduction can’t exceed the net profit from that business for certain loss rules; in your case, with $15,000 gross and $650 business expenses, the allocated home‑office of ~$4,174 is likely allowable because your net self‑employment income before SE tax would remain positive. If you prefer simplicity or expect small benefit, the $500 simplified method is fine.

2) Self‑employment tax and how business deductions help
- Your contracting income is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net self‑employment earnings). You calculate SE tax on net profit (gross minus business deductions including home office).
- You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).
- Any legitimate business expenses related to contracting (software, supplies, phone portion, mileage) are deductible on Schedule C.

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($650) — when deductible
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job: under current tax law (TCJA, through 2025) miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers — so you generally cannot deduct them.
- If they are business expenses connected to your self‑employment work, deduct them on Schedule C.

