Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical rundown of how your 2024 items are treated, what’s likely best, the forms you’ll need, and next steps. If you want, I can run rough numbers for your taxable income once you confirm a couple of missing details below.

1) What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment (IT contracting) business expenses
  - Your contracting income and any ordinary and necessary expenses for that work (equipment, software, supplies, advertising, phone/Internet portion used for business, mileage, and the home-office deduction) are deductible on Schedule C (reduces your self-employment net income).
  - The $650 unreimbursed expenses: deductible only if they were business expenses related to your contracting work. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible (suspended under current law).
- Home-office
  - Because you have self-employment income, you may take a home-office deduction for the space used exclusively and regularly for your contracting.
  - Two methods: simplified (up to $5/sq ft, max 300 sq ft) or actual-expense method (pro-rate rent, utilities, insurance, etc., by business-use percentage).
  - Your numbers: 100 sq ft exclusive / 690 sq ft total = 14.49% business-use. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 → rent portion for home office ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174. That alone is larger than the simplified-method amount (100 × $5 = $500), so the actual-expense method will likely give a bigger deduction if you have no unusual complications. Use Form 8829 (or report on Schedule C — simplified method is claimed directly on Schedule C).
- Student-loan interest ($3,600)
  - The student-loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) applies only to interest paid in the tax year; you cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on a 2024 return.
  - The deduction phases out above certain MAGI thresholds (single filers). Given your approximate income level (W-2 + contracting + interest ≈ $101k), you will likely be partially or fully phased out from claiming the student-loan interest deduction. Exact eligibility depends on your MAGI; we’ll need to compute MAGI to be certain.
- Charitable contributions ($2,000)
  - Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep your receipt (you have it).
- Interest income ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115)
  - Report on Form 1040 (you’ll receive 1099-INTs).
- Self-employment tax
  - Net self-employment income (Schedule C) is subject to self-employment tax (~15.3%). You pay this via Schedule SE; half of the self-employment tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (practical guidance)
- For 2024 the single standard deduction increased (IRS inflation adjustments). With only $2,000 in charitable donations shown and assuming no mortgage interest, large property taxes, or large state & local taxes, your likely itemized deductions look small and will probably be less than the standard deduction — so you’ll very likely take the standard deduction.
- Important: Home-office and most business expenses reduce your business income on Schedule C (therefore lower your AGI) but do NOT count toward Schedule A itemized deductions. So the home-office deduction helps your tax bill even if you take the standard deduction.
- To be certain, I need any of the following you might have: mortgage interest paid, property tax paid, state income tax paid (or state tax withheld shown on your W-2), and any other itemizable expenses (medical, large miscellaneous—rare). If combined Schedule A items exceed the standard deduction, then itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

3) How to treat the home office (recommended for your facts)
- You’re self-employed for those contracting gigs, so you may take the home-office deduction.
- Recommendation: use the actual-expense method because your rent-based allocation (~$4,174) is larger than the simplified-method amount ($500).
- Keep records: square footage measurements, lease/rent amount, dates, utility bills, renter’s insurance, and any other home-related expenses you allocate.
- Use Form 8829 to calculate the deduction (or report on Schedule C if simplified). The deduction reduces your Schedule C profit and therefore reduces income tax and self-employment tax.

4) Forms you’ll need
- Form 1040 — main return.
- W-2 — TechStart Inc.
- 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (or records) for contracting income (you should receive 1099s from each client if > $600).
- 1099-INT — Chase and Charles Schwab interest income.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your contracting income and business expenses (including home-office deduction as a business expense).
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax and the deductible portion of SE tax.
