- Self-employment tax: on net self-employment profit you’ll owe self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute it on Schedule SE; one-half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- The decision is “standard deduction” vs the sum of Schedule A itemized deductions (charity, state & local taxes, mortgage interest, medical above threshold, etc.). From the items you listed, your only clear Schedule A item is $2,000 charitable giving — that’s well below the single filer standard deduction for 2024. So, based on the data you gave, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for your federal return. (Student loan interest and the self-employment-related deductions reduce your AGI regardless of whether you itemize, so they don’t affect this standard vs itemize choice directly.) If you have additional deductible items (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, real property taxes, large medical expenses), re-check the comparison.  

Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction for your contracting income (Form 8995 or 8995-A). QBI is calculated after certain adjustments, and the deduction depends on taxable income thresholds and the nature of the business. With modest self-employment income ($15k before expenses) you will likely be able to claim some QBI deduction. Tax software or a preparer can calculate this for you.

State (California) considerations
- You’ll file California Form 540. California generally follows federal treatment for business income and home-office deduction (with some differences). State standard deduction/itemize thresholds are different; however, your federal itemizable amounts still look small, so you will likely take the CA standard deduction as well. If you want I can summarize CA-specific differences once you confirm any additional state items (property tax paid, state tax withheld, etc.).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — student loan interest adjustment, deduction for half of SE tax, etc.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (home office, $650 business expenses if they are business-related, other expenses).  
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — computes SE tax on net Schedule C profit.  
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for QBI deduction (if applicable)  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t appear beneficial from the items given)  
- Schedule B — only if interest income > $1,500 (not needed here)  

Documents to collect / records to keep
- W-2 from TechStart Inc.  
- 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for contracting income (or records if you received payments without 1099s). Report all income even without 1099s.  
- 1099-INT (if issued) from Chase and Charles Schwab — or statements showing the interest you received.  
- Rent receipts/lease and records for utilities/insurance if you use actual home-office method.  
- MOHELA or lender Form 1098-E for student loan interest paid in 2024 (or printout of interest paid if 1098-E not issued).  
- Charitable donation receipt for Northwest Harvest.  
- Receipts/invoices for the $650 business expenses.  

Practical next steps and rough recommendation
1. Confirm whether the $3,600 student-loan interest was paid in 2024 and whether the $650 and home-office relate to your contracting work.  
2. If the $650 and home-office are for your contracting work: report the $15k on Schedule C and claim business expenses (home office actual method likely gives a larger deduction because of the rent allocation, but use whichever method is correct and best with documentation). This will lower taxable/self-employment income and SE tax.  
3. Take the federal standard deduction for 2024 (almost certainly beneficial given only $2,000 of itemizable charitable deductions reported). You’ll still get the student-loan interest adjustment (up to $2,500) and the business-related home-office deduction even if you take the standard deduction.  
4. Consider tax software or a preparer for precise calculations (SE tax, QBI will affect taxable income and can be a little tricky). I can run a rough tax estimate if you want — I’d need confirmation on the questions above and whether to use the actual or simplified home-office method.

