Thanks — I can walk you through how to treat each piece and what you’ll need to file. A few quick clarifying questions first (they change some results):

- Is the $3,600 of student-loan interest interest you actually paid in tax year 2024, or is that a cumulative amount from 2016–2019? (You can only deduct interest paid during the tax year and you should have a 1098‑E from the servicer for 2024.)
- The $650 — is that unreimbursed business expense you personally paid while doing the IT contracting (i.e., a self‑employment expense), or is it an unreimbursed employee expense from a W‑2 job?
- Do you have any other itemizable items (state income tax withheld or paid, property tax, mortgage interest, other charitable gifts, medical expenses)?

Below is a clear summary of what you can (and usually cannot) deduct, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, and which forms to use.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment (contracting) business expenses
  - The $15,000 contracting income is self‑employment income. Legitimate business expenses (supplies, software, travel, the $650 if it’s a contracting expense) are deductible against that income on Schedule C.
  - If those $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your federal return (miscellaneous employee expense deduction was eliminated).

- Home‑office
  - Allowed only if you use the space regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment/contracting business (not for your W‑2 job).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For your 100 sq ft that would be $500.
    - Regular (actual expenses): calculate the business percentage of the home (100 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%). Then multiply that percentage by qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). Example using your rent only: $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 rent × 14.4927% ≈ $4,176 of home‑office deduction (you could also add a prorated share of utilities etc. if you pay them). If you use the regular method you normally complete Form 8829 (or report per Schedule C rules).
  - Home‑office deductions reduce your Schedule C net profit (so they reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax).

- Self‑employment tax deduction
  - You pay self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). Half of the self‑employment tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (reported on Schedule 1) and reduces your AGI.

- Student‑loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to income (MAGI) phaseouts. If your MAGI is above the phaseout range, the deduction is reduced or eliminated. You must have interest paid in 2024 and a 1098‑E from the loan servicer.

- Charitable contributions ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest)
  - Cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt / acknowledgement.
  - If you take the standard deduction you cannot also claim this on Schedule A.

2) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- You should compare the sum of your itemizable deductions (Schedule A) to the standard deduction for a single filer.
- Itemizable items commonly include: charitable gifts, state and local taxes paid (SALT limited to $10,000), mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses (only the portion above the threshold), etc.
- Based on the information you gave (only $2,000 charitable plus whatever state taxes you paid), it is likely the standard deduction will be larger than your Schedule A total, so you’ll probably take the standard deduction — meaning the $2,000 charitable gift would not reduce your federal taxable income. (Home‑office deductions are NOT on Schedule A — they reduce business income on Schedule C whether or not you itemize.)
- If you want, I can run a quick comparison if you tell me your state tax paid or withheld and any other itemizable amounts.

3) How the home office is treated (recap + recommendation)
- Only deductible for self‑employed/independent contractor activity. It reduces net self‑employment income.
- Choose simplified ($500 for your 100 sq ft) if you want simplicity, or use the actual method (Form 8829) if actual allocated expenses (rent + utilities etc.) give a larger deduction (in your numbers the actual rent allocation (~$4,176) is substantially larger than the $500 simplified amount).
- Keep records showing exclusive regular business use of the 100 sq ft, and receipts for rent, utilities, insurance, etc.

4) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student‑loan interest (and to report other income items)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (including the home‑office deduction if you use the simplified method or if you report a simple line; for the full allocation use Form 8829)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use if you choose the regular/actual method for the home office
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on net Schedule C profit
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (to claim the $2,000 charitable gift and any state taxes, mortgage interest, etc.)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction for pass‑through/self‑employment income. Whether you qualify and which form depends on taxable income and the type of services.
- State return: California Form 540 (CA has different rules in some respects; check CA conformity for certain adjustments).

5) Practical numbers and example (rough estimate so you see the effect)
- Total income: W‑2 $84,900 + interest $1,115 + gross contracting $15,000 = $101,015.
- If you use the regular home‑office allocation of rent (~$4,176) and your $650 contracting expense is legitimate business expense:
  - Schedule C expenses ≈ $4,176 + $650 = $4,826 → net self‑employment income ≈ $10,174.
  - SE tax roughly 15.3% on ~92.35% of that → about $1,400–$1,500 SE tax; half of that (~$700–$750) is an adjustment to income.
  - Student‑loan interest: you can only deduct interest paid in 2024 up to $2,500 and subject to MAGI phaseout.
  - Charitable $2,000 only helps if you itemize.
- With those numbers, you’re likely better off taking the standard deduction (single filers’ standard deduction is typically in the mid‑$10k to mid‑$10k+ range; confirm the exact 2024 standard deduction amount). If you want I can compute a precise taxable income estimate once you confirm the student interest year and any other itemized amounts.

6) Documentation to gather
- W‑2 from TechStart Inc.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC or records for the $15,000 contracting income.
- 1099‑INT for Chase and Charles Schwab interest (or records).
- 1098‑E from MOHELA for any student interest paid in 2024 (if applicable).
- Rent receipts / lease and utility bills to support the home office allocation.
- Receipts for the $650 business expense and the $2,000 charitable gift (written acknowledgement from Northwest Harvest).
- Any records of state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest statements (1098) if any, property tax receipts.

7) Final notes and offer
- State taxes: California mostly follows federal for business expense treatment but there are differences; you’ll file Form 540 for CA and may have small differences for adjustments — I can help with state-specific points if you want.
- Not a substitute for a CPA: this is guidance based on typical federal rules; if your situation has any complications (e.g., you were also a W‑2 employee with unreimbursed expenses, or you had reimbursed employee expenses, or big other itemized items), consider verifying with a tax pro.
- Next steps I can do now:
  - If you confirm whether the $3,600 is paid in 2024 and whether the $650 is a contracting expense, I can run the arithmetic and give an estimated federal taxable income and estimated federal tax + self‑employment tax, and show the itemize vs standard comparison.
  - Or I can prepare a checklist of exactly what to give to a tax preparer.

Which would you like me to do next — run the estimate using the regular home‑office method, run it using the simplified method for comparison, or just compare itemize vs standard if you give any additional itemizable amounts?

