Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — that’s enough to give a clear, practical plan. I’ll (1) summarize which items are deductible and how they’re treated, (2) run through a quick estimate of the key numbers, (3) recommend whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) list the forms you’ll need, and (5) ask a couple short clarifying questions so the answer can be tightened.

1) How each item you listed is treated
- W-2 from TechStart Inc ($84,900): ordinary wage income on Form 1040. Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are NOT deductible for most taxpayers (suspended by TCJA through 2025), so the $650 is not deductible unless it was for your contracting work (see below).
- Contracting income ($15,000 from 3 clients): treated as self‑employment income. Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including part of your rent for a home office, supplies, business mileage, the portion of the $650 if it was business-related, etc.). You’ll also owe self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE).
- Interest income ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115): taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs).
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusively out of 690 sq ft total; rent $2,400/month):
  - If the office is used exclusively and regularly for the self‑employment contracting, you can claim a home‑office deduction on Schedule C.
  - Two methods:
    - Regular method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses. Your office is 100/690 = 14.4928% of the home. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800; rent portion deductible = 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,172. You can also deduct a prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable. Use Form 8829 to compute this if you use the regular method.
    - Simplified method: $5 × square feet up to 300 sq ft → 100 × $5 = $500. Since your calculated rent portion (~$4,172) is larger, the regular method will likely give a bigger deduction unless you have no other allocable expenses.
  - The home‑office deduction for self‑employed is limited to net income from the business (excess may carry forward).
- Student loan interest ($3,600 / MOHELA 2016–2019): only interest actually paid during the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible (up to $2,500) as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1. There is a MAGI phaseout (single taxpayers lose the deduction above the phaseout range). I need to clarify if the $3,600 is interest you paid in 2024 (or is that an aggregate from past years).
- Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize. Keep the receipt. (There is no routine above‑the‑line deduction for cash contributions to charity for 2024 for most taxpayers.)
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650): if these were expenses related to your W‑2 employer job, they are not deductible (suspended). If they were business expenses for the contracting work, report them on Schedule C.

2) Quick rough numbers / tax-impact estimates (rounded)
Assumptions: home‑office regular method used, the $650 is a business expense, the $3,600 student loan interest was paid in tax year 2024 and is within MAGI limits. If any assumptions are different, results change.

- W‑2 wages: 84,900
- Interest income: 1,115
- Self‑employment gross: 15,000
- Schedule C business deductions:
  - Home office (rent portion): ≈ 4,172
  - Other business expenses (including your $650): 650
  => Net self‑employment income ≈ 15,000 − 4,822 = 10,178

- Self‑employment tax (approx): taxable portion = 10,178 × 92.35% ≈ 9,399; SE tax ≈ 9,399 × 15.3% ≈ $1,438. Half of that (~$719) is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (possible): roughly 20% × QBI (QBI ≈ net business income after adjustments) → about 20% × 10,178 ≈ $2,036 (subject to overall taxable income limits and rules).
- Student loan interest adjustment (if eligible): up to $2,500 (but phaseouts apply).
- Charitable: $2,000 (only if itemizing).

Adjusted gross income (very rough):
- AGI start: wages 84,900 + interest 1,115 + net SE 10,178 = 96,193
- Less adjustments: half SE tax ~719; student loan interest up to 2,500 (if eligible) => AGI reduction ~3,219 => AGI ≈ 92,974 (very approximate — depends on exact student loan allowance).

Itemized deductions available that you mentioned: charitable $2,000. (You didn’t list mortgage interest, state income tax / property tax amounts; those could add to itemized totals, but remember SALT cap $10,000.)
Standard deduction (single, tax year 2024): $14,600.

Given only the $2,000 charitable contribution (and no significant other itemized items provided), the standard deduction ($14,600) will almost certainly be larger — so you will likely take the standard deduction.

Bottom line: the home office and business expenses reduce your self‑employment income regardless of whether you itemize. The student loan interest (if paid in 2024 and under the MAGI limits) is an above‑the‑line deduction and helps whether or not you itemize. The charitable donation helps only if you itemize, and based on the numbers you provided, itemizing probably doesn’t beat the standard deduction.

3) Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual return)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest, half of SE tax, etc.)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) – to report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to compute home office deduction (not used for the simplified method)
  - Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995 is the simpler form often used if under threshold)
  - Form 1099‑INTs (information returns from banks) and any 1099‑NEC you received from clients (you still report the income even if you didn’t get a 1099)
  - Form 1040‑ES: for quarterly estimated tax payments if you need to make them for 2024 (or for next year if you expect similar self‑employment income)
- State:
  - California Form 540 (or the correct CA 2024 individual return form)
  - California may have its own rules for student loan interest and QBI treatment (check CA specifics).

4) Practical recommendations / next steps
- Likely take the standard deduction ($14,600) unless you have additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, large medical expenses, etc.).
- For the home office I recommend the regular method (Form 8829) in your case because the rent‑portion deduction (~$4,172) is larger than the simplified $500. Keep clear records (floorplan, measurements, rent statements, utility bills).
- Put the contracting income and business expenses on Schedule C. Claim the self‑employment tax on Schedule SE and the half‑SE tax adjustment on Schedule 1.
- If you paid student loan interest in 2024, confirm how much was paid in 2024 and whether your MAGI is below the phaseout range; if eligible, you can deduct up to $2,500 on Schedule 1 (above the line).
- Keep receipts for the $2,000 charity (written acknowledgement).
- If you expect similar self‑employment income for 2025, you may need to make quarterly estimated payments (use Form 1040‑ES or pay via IRS Direct Pay) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If you want a more precise tax estimate (income tax + self‑employment tax + QBI effect), I can compute a line‑by‑line estimate — I’ll need any of these additional items:
  - Exact amount of student loan interest paid in 2024 (not prior years)
  - Whether the $650 unreimbursed expenses were business-related (contracting) or employee W‑2 related
  - Any state income tax withheld / property taxes / mortgage interest paid in 2024
  - Any 1099‑NEC amounts you received, or amounts withheld/estimated payments already made

5) Quick questions so I can finalize exact numbers
- Was the $3,600 student loan interest actually paid in 2024, or is it interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019)? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your contracting business (so they belong on Schedule C) or were they employee expenses for the W‑2 job?
- Do you have mortgage interest, state income tax / property tax amounts, or other deductible expenses not already listed?

If you want, I can:
- run a more detailed estimated tax calculation (federal + CA) if you answer the 3 questions above, or
- prepare a checklist of documents to collect for filing or for a tax preparer.

