Short summary / bottom line
- You should report the $15,000 of contracting income on Schedule C and will owe self‑employment tax on the net profit. That also lets you deduct business expenses (including an allocable portion of rent for a qualifying home office) on Schedule C.
- The home‑office deduction is available for your contracting work (if the 100 sq ft is used regularly and exclusively for that business). Using the actual‑expense method will likely give a much larger deduction than the simplified method for your rent.
- With the income numbers you gave, you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing — so your $2,000 cash donation alone won’t force you to itemize.
- The “student loan interest” you reported needs clarification — only interest paid during the tax year (2024) is deductible (up to $2,500 subject to income phase‑outs). With your likely MAGI you will probably be above the phaseout and not eligible for the student‑loan deduction for 2024, but I’ll explain how to check.

1) What you can deduct now (based on the details you gave)
- Contracting (self‑employment) business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. This includes:
  - Home office (if the space is used exclusively and regularly for the contracting business).
  - The $650 unreimbursed expenses — if those are business expenses for your contracting work (supplies, mileage, phone/Internet portion, software, etc.) they belong on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Charitable cash contributions: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You have $2,000 and a receipt — keep that for your records.
- Student loan interest: only interest actually paid in 2024 may be deductible (up to $2,500), subject to MAGI limits for single filers (there is a phaseout). If your MAGI > the phaseout top limit for 2024, you cannot take this deduction.
- Interest income from Chase and Schwab ($920 + $195 = $1,115): taxable and reported on Form 1040. (Schedule B only required if interest/dividend total > $1,500.)

2) Home‑office: two methods and sample calculations
You said home is 690 sq ft, home office 100 sq ft exclusive. Percent business use = 100 / 690 = 14.4928% (≈ 14.49%).

A) Simplified method:
- IRS allows $5 per sq ft (up to 300 sq ft) for a deduction. For 100 sq ft => $500 deduction. You enter this on Schedule C (you do NOT file Form 8829 with the simplified method).

B) Actual expense method:
- You can allocate a share of actual rent and qualifying home expenses (rent, renter’s insurance, utilities, repairs attributable to the office) to the business.
- Using only the rent you gave ($2,400/month → $28,800/year), the business portion is:
  - 28,800 × (100/690) ≈ $4,174 (this is just the rent share; add any allocable portion of utilities, insurance, repairs).
- That $4,174 (plus portion of other home expenses) is claimed on Form 8829 and carried to Schedule C.
- Important: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business. If it does, any unused deduction can carry forward to future years.

Given your rent amount, the actual method will almost certainly produce a much larger deduction than the simplified method.

3) Effect on taxable income / example rough calculations
(These are illustrative, using the numbers you gave and assuming the $650 and home office are business expenses related to the contracting work.)

- Option A (simplified home office)
  - Contracting gross: $15,000
