  - Self‑employment tax (approx): use 92.35% × net profit = 13,850 × 0.9235 = 12,796 × 15.3% ≈ $1,958 SE tax
  - Half SE tax deductible (above‑the‑line) ≈ $979
  - AGI ≈ 84,900 (W‑2) + 13,850 (Schedule C net) + 1,115 (interest) − 979 (half SE tax) = ≈ $98,886

- Option B (actual home office using rent share)
  - Home office portion of rent ≈ $4,174 + $650 = business expenses total ≈ $4,824
  - Schedule C net = 15,000 − 4,824 = $10,176
  - SE tax: 10,176 × 0.9235 ≈ 9,399 × 15.3% ≈ $1,437; half SE tax ≈ $719
  - AGI ≈ 84,900 + 10,176 + 1,115 − 719 = ≈ $95,472

Either way, your AGI will be roughly $95k–$99k in this scenario. That level of income makes you unlikely to qualify for the student‑loan interest deduction because it phases out for single filers (the phaseout starts and ends well below your AGI here). I can check exact MAGI phaseout thresholds if you want the IRS table referenced.

4) Itemize vs Standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction for single filers is (use current IRS amount) — in practice, with only $2,000 charitable contributions plus whatever state tax you paid, your itemized deductions are very likely well below the standard deduction.
- Therefore, take the standard deduction unless you have other significant deductible expenses (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, high medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, large charitable giving, etc.).
- If you do take the standard deduction, charitable contributions are not separately deductible (unless you do qualified charitable distributions from IRAs or other special provisions).

5) Other items you should plan for
- Self‑employment tax: net Schedule C profit is subject to SE tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of profit). Factor this into withholding/estimated payments.
- Estimated tax payments: If you didn’t have sufficient withholding to cover the extra income and SE tax, you will likely owe tax and possibly underpayment penalties unless you made estimated payments or had enough withholding. Consider increasing W‑2 withholding or making quarterly estimated payments for 2025.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C business (calculated on Form 8995 or 8995‑A), which can reduce taxable income. With your numbers, you will probably qualify for some QBI deduction — I can estimate it if you want.
- Retirement contributions: you may be able to reduce taxable income and SE tax by contributing to a SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k), or traditional IRA (limits and rules apply). Good option to reduce tax if you want to save for retirement.

6) Forms you will likely need (Federal)
