Thanks — useful detail. I can walk you through what’s likely deductible, whether itemizing will help, how to treat the home office, and the forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions first (will change the answers if the answers are different):

- Is the $3,600 listed as “student loan interest (MOHELA, 2016–2019)” interest you actually paid in tax year 2024, or is that total interest from earlier years? (Only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible.)
- Are the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses you paid as part of the IT contracting work (i.e., business expenses for your self‑employment income), or are they unreimbursed expenses relating to your W‑2 job?
- Are you renting (you said $2,400/month) and therefore do not have mortgage interest/property tax? Do you know how much state tax was withheld on your W‑2 or how much state estimated tax you paid in 2024?

If you prefer, answer those and I’ll refine the numbers. Below is the general guidance and an illustrative calculation using the numbers you gave.

1) Which items are deductible (and how)
- W‑2 wages (TechStart Inc., $84,900): reported on Form 1040 (Box 1 from Form W‑2).
- Self‑employment income ($15,000 from contracting): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Business expenses related to that contracting are deductible on Schedule C (including the home‑office share if you qualify).
- Interest income ($920 Chase, $195 Schwab = $1,115): reported on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV or similar).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment to income (you don’t have to itemize). This deduction is limited by income (phase‑outs apply). You must have actually paid the interest in the tax year and the loan must be a qualified student loan. Confirm whether the $3,600 is paid in 2024; if so the maximum deductible is $2,500 (subject to phaseout).
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize (on Schedule A). Keep the receipt; cash gifts to public charities are deductible.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible for W‑2 employees (suspended since 2018). If the $650 are business expenses for your contracting work, they belong on Schedule C (not Schedule A). If they are truly unreimbursed expenses of a W‑2 employee, they are no longer deductible for most taxpayers.
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive use; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month):
  - Only available if you use the space "regularly and exclusively" for business AND you are self‑employed (Schedule C) or meet a very narrow exception. It’s not available to general W‑2 employees.
  - You can choose the simplified method ($5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 for the year) OR the actual expense method (allocate a portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs etc. by the business percentage).
  - Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. With annual rent $2,400 × 12 = $28,800, the rent portion allocable to business ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,170. Add allocable utilities/etc. as applicable. The actual method usually gives a bigger deduction here, but you must keep records and complete Form 8829 (or the Schedule C workpaper) to compute it.

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Self‑employment income after Schedule C expenses is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net SE income). You report SE tax on Schedule SE.
- One half of your SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (reduces your AGI).
- The home‑office deduction and other Schedule C expenses reduce net profit and therefore both income tax and SE tax.

3) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- The standard deduction for single taxpayers is significantly larger than it used to be (and is adjusted for inflation each year). Unless you have large mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, or sizable other itemizable items, most taxpayers with only ~$2,000 of charitable donations will be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Your charitable ($2,000) + any other itemizable items (state tax withheld, property tax if any, medical beyond threshold) would have to exceed the standard deduction before you should itemize.
- Important: the student loan interest deduction is an adjustment above the line regardless of whether you itemize, so you don’t need to itemize to get that (if you’re eligible).

