- W‑2 wages: 84,900
- Contracting: 15,000
- Interest: 1,115
Total gross = 101,015

Step B — Schedule C business math (example):
- Gross contracting income: 15,000
- Business expenses: home‑office alloc. ≈ 4,174 (rent share) + other business expenses 650 = 4,824
- Net profit ≈ 10,176

Step C — self‑employment tax (approx):
- SE taxable base ≈ 10,176 × 0.9235 ≈ 9,400
- SE tax ≈ 9,400 × 15.3% ≈ $1,440
- Above‑the‑line deduction (half SE tax) ≈ $720

Step D — AGI and adjustments (approx):
- Total income 101,015 minus Schedule C expenses 4,824 = 96,191
- Subtract half SE tax 720 → AGI before student loan interest ≈ 95,471
- Student loan interest deduction (max $2,500 if you qualify; phased out based on MAGI) would reduce AGI further.

Step E — deduction choice:
- If you take standard deduction (single), your taxable income = AGI − standard deduction.
- If you itemize, your itemized deductions would at minimum include $2,000 charitable and any state tax paid (capped by $10,000 SALT), etc. With only $2,000 charity and no mortgage interest, itemizing is unlikely to beat the standard deduction.

Bottom line on itemizing: With only a $2,000 charitable gift and no mortgage interest or large state taxes shown here, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. The home office and contractor expenses reduce your self‑employment income whether you itemize or not (because they’re on Schedule C).

4) Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — used for certain items like student loan interest if applicable
  - Schedule C (self‑employment income and expenses)
  - Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)
  - Form 8829 if using the actual home‑office method (to figure allowable home office deduction). If you use the simplified method ($5/sq ft), you do not file Form 8829; report the simplified amount on Schedule C.
  - Schedule A (only if you decide to itemize)
- Information returns to collect/expect:
  - Form W‑2 from TechStart
  - 1099‑NEC or records of the $15,000 contracting income (even if you did not receive 1099s)
  - 1099‑INT from Chase and 1099‑DIV/1099‑INT from Charles Schwab
  - 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest paid (or your payment records if 1098‑E isn’t issued)
- State: California Form 540 (resident) and any CA schedules; pay attention to state withholding and any estimated payments.

5) Other practical items and recommendations
- Keep receipts/proof: charitable receipt for Northwest Harvest, receipts/invoices for business expenses, records showing exclusive home‑office use, 1098‑E (student loan interest), and 1099s.
- If the contracting is continuing or will increase, consider quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Consider the simplified vs actual home‑office method: simplified is easier ($500 in your case) but actual appears larger (~$4k of rent allocation alone). The actual method reduces net SE profit (and therefore SE tax); use Form 8829 for actual.
- If student loan interest was not paid in 2024 (or interest was paid in earlier years), it cannot be deducted now — only interest paid during the tax year is deductible.
- If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are W‑2 employee expenses, they’re generally nondeductible. If they’re business expenses for your contracting, deduct them on Schedule C.
- Consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer (or reputable tax software) to ensure you maximize allowable deductions and correctly prepare Schedule C / Form 8829 and handle the student loan phaseout rules.

