Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I’ll walk through each item, show rough numbers so you can see the effect, and list the forms you’ll need. I’ll call out assumptions I made; if you want more precision, tell me any missing numbers (state tax withheld, whether the $650 and the home-office expenses are for the contracting work, whether you received 1099-NEC forms, etc.).

Quick summary
- Likely take the standard deduction (your itemizable items shown here are well under the standard deduction).
- Your contracting income is reported on Schedule C; you can take a home-office deduction and other business expenses on Schedule C. That reduces self-employment tax and income tax.
- Student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out at higher MAGI; with the numbers you gave, you’ll probably be phased out and unable to claim it.
- Charitable gift ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize (so probably not deductible for federal if you take the standard deduction).
- Key forms: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (regular home office), or use the simplified home-office method and report on Schedule C; Schedule A only if you itemize; keep Forms 1099-NEC / 1099-INT / 1098-E.

Detailed walkthrough and calculations (estimates)
Assumptions from your message:
- W-2 wages from TechStart: $84,900
- Self-employed (contracting) gross receipts: $15,000 (3 clients)
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year for a 690 sq ft rental unit
- Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive use
- Business (contracting) expenses you reported: $650 (I assume these are deductible business expenses)
- Student loan interest paid: $3,600 (years 2016–2019 payments to MOHELA — you’ll need Form 1098-E from the servicer showing interest paid that’s deductible for the tax year; only interest paid during 2024 counts toward a 2024 return)
- Charitable cash gift: $2,000 with receipt

1) Home office — two methods, with numbers
Home office percent = 100 / 690 = 14.49% (≈14.5%)

a) Regular method (allocate actual home expenses)
- Annual rent = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,175 deductible as business expense (this is the main number; you could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. similarly if you have them)
- If you take the regular method and have only rent and the $650 other business expense:
  - Total business expenses = $4,175 + $650 = $4,825
  - Net self-employment income = $15,000 − $4,825 = $10,175

b) Simplified method
- $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829)
- Net self-employment income if simplified = $15,000 − ($650 + $500) = $13,850

Which to pick?
- Regular method yields a larger deduction here (~$4,175 vs $500), so regular method is likely better. The regular-method computation is done on Form 8829 and then flows into Schedule C.

Limit on home-office deduction
- The allowable home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business (before the home-office deduction), so since your contracting is $15k gross, the $4,175 doesn't exceed the business profit after other expenses in this example.

2) Self-employment tax (estimate, using the regular method)
- Net SE income (regular method example): ≈ $10,175
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × net ≈ $1,438
- Deductible half of SE tax (an adjustment to income) ≈ $719

3) How this affects AGI and the student loan interest deduction
- Income components: W-2 $84,900 + net SE (≈ $10,175) + interest $1,115 = ~ $96,190 total income (before adjustments)
- Subtract half SE tax (~$719) → estimated AGI ≈ $95,471
- Student loan interest deduction: maximum federal deduction is $2,500 and is an adjustment to income, but there are phaseouts based on MAGI (single filers: phased out as income rises). With an AGI around $95k, you are likely above the phaseout range and therefore probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction. (Exact phaseout thresholds change with inflation; if you want I can check current IRS thresholds or recompute AGI with alternative home-office choices/retirement contributions that could bring you under the phaseout.)

4) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Your likely itemized deductions here (just the $2k charity plus any state/local taxes you paid) are well below the standard deduction for single filers — so most likely you will take the standard deduction and not get a separate federal tax benefit for that $2,000. (If you have large state taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t mention, that can change the analysis.)

5) Unreimbursed expenses $650
- If those $650 are business expenses for your contracting work, they go on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed expenses as an employee (W-2 job), they are generally not deductible for federal returns under current law (suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Based on the items you listed (only $2,000 charity + likely not much else), the standard deduction for a single filer is substantially larger than your likely itemized total. So take the standard deduction.
- If you have additional itemizable amounts (state & local taxes up to $10k cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), re-run the comparison. If your state income tax or property tax paid is large, that could push you to itemize.

7) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income) — student loan interest adjustment if you qualify; also other adjustments go here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the regular method for home-office deduction (if you use simplified method, you generally don’t file Form 8829; you report the simplified-method deduction on Schedule C)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable donations, SALT, mortgage interest, etc.)
- You should receive/need:
  - Form(s) 1099-NEC (from each client who paid you $600+; if you didn’t get them, you still report the income)
  - Form(s) 1099-INT (from Chase and Schwab for interest)
  - Form 1098-E (student loan interest paid in 2024) from MOHELA if you paid interest in 2024 and intend to claim the deduction
- For state return: California Form 540 (and CA has some differences; check CA guidance — many federal provisions carry over but there are exceptions)

8) Other items and planning tips
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040-ES) to avoid underpayment penalties if you expect tax to be due beyond what your W-2 withholding covers.
- Retirement contributions (SEP-IRA, Solo 401(k), Traditional IRA): contributing to a tax-deductible retirement plan for self-employment income can reduce AGI and may (a) reduce your taxable income and (b) possibly allow you to qualify for certain phaseout-limited deductions. SEP-IRA contribution limits are based on net SE income (complex calculation) — could be worth discussing with a tax pro.
- Keep records: save receipts for the $2,000 donation, the $650 business expenses, documentation for the home office (floor plan or note about exclusive use), rent statements showing monthly rent, 1099s, 1098-E, and all bank interest statements.

Limitations / caveats
- I made several assumptions (that the $650 is a business expense, that the $3,600 in student loan interest paid in earlier years is not relevant unless you paid interest in 2024 — only interest paid during the tax year is deductible). Also phaseout thresholds and standard deduction amounts change yearly — I used approximate thresholds and showed how to estimate your AGI. For exact tax liability and to confirm CA-specific rules, consult a CPA or tax preparer or I can compute a more detailed estimate if you provide any missing numbers (e.g., exact 2024 student interest paid, state tax paid or withheld, whether the $650 is business-related, any additional deductible expenses or retirement contributions).

Would you like me to:
- Recompute the numbers if the $650 is not business-related (or if there are other business expenses)?
- Compute a more exact tax estimate (federal tax) including an estimate of whether you’ll owe taxes / need to make estimated payments?
- Draft a checklist email you can send to a tax preparer with all necessary documents?

