Short answer up front
- Likely best to take the standard deduction for 2024 (single = $14,600) — your itemizable deductions (charity $2,000 plus any state taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t mention) are probably smaller than the standard deduction.
- Claim your contracting income and home-office costs on Schedule C (or Form 8829 for actual home-office allocation). The home-office deduction looks beneficial (actual method gives ~ $4,174 vs simplified $500).
- Student‑loan interest is limited by income phaseouts and is likely fully phased out given your income — you probably cannot claim the $3,600 as a deduction for 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if from a W‑2 job) are not deductible (TCJA suspension through 2025). If the $650 relates to your contracting work, it’s deductible on Schedule C.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if using actual home‑office method), 1099‑NEC (or records of 1099s), 1099‑INT, 1098‑E (student loan interest statement if available), and CA Form 540 for state filing.

Details and quick estimate
1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $84,900 (TechStart)
- Self‑employment / contracting: $15,000 (report on Schedule C; you should receive 1099‑NEC if each client paid ≥$600)
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 (reported on 1099‑INT)
Total gross income ≈ $101,015.

2) Home‑office deduction (you qualify for self‑employed business use)
- Home = 690 sq ft, office = 100 sq ft → office = 100/690 ≈ 14.493%
- Rent = $2,400/mo → annual rent $28,800
- Actual-share of rent = 14.493% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174 (you can include a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. if you have them)
- Simplified method = $5 × 100 sq ft = $500
=> Actual method looks better here (≈ $4,174) if you have records and no complicated depreciation issues.

3) Treatment of the $650 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job — not deductible (suspended under current law for 2018–2025).
- If these are expenses related to your contracting/self‑employment work (supplies, mileage, phone, etc.), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your business profit.

4) Student‑loan interest ($3,600)
- Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible, claimed as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1/Form 1040). If the $3,600 refers to interest paid in 2016–2019, that is not deductible on your 2024 return.
- There is an income phaseout for the student loan interest deduction. Given your estimated modified AGI (roughly mid‑$90k in my estimate), you are above the typical phaseout range for single filers, so the deduction is likely reduced to $0. You should confirm exact MAGI and the 2024 phaseout thresholds, but plan as if it’s not deductible.

5) Self‑employment tax and AGI effects (rough estimate)
- If you take the actual home‑office and the $650 is a business expense:
  - Net self‑employment income ≈ $15,000 − $4,174 − $650 = $10,176.
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% applied to 92.35% of net ≈ $1,438 (Schedule SE).
  - Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $719 (above‑the‑line deduction).
- Rough AGI after these adjustments ≈ $95,470; minus the standard deduction $14,600 → taxable income ≈ $80,870 (this is an estimate — not a return).

6) Itemize vs standard
- You only listed $2,000 charitable giving as an itemizable deduction (and student interest likely disallowed). Unless you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other itemizable items that add up to more than $14,600, the standard deduction will be larger. So you will likely take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report contracting income & business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable gifts, state taxes, mortgage interest, etc.)
- 1099‑NEC / records of payments from clients
- 1099‑INTs for bank/broker interest
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if issued and if you think you qualify
- California: Form 540 (state return) — CA has its own rules; some deductions differ from federal

Practical next steps / documents to gather
- Confirm whether the $3,600 student interest was paid in 2024; get 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2024 (if > $600).
- Confirm whether the $650 are contracting-related. If yes, list receipts and what they were for.
- Collect 1099‑NEC from each client (or your records showing payments if no 1099 issued).
- 1099‑INTs from Chase and Schwab.
- Rent receipts/lease showing monthly rent; utility bills if you’ll claim shared utilities.
- Receipts for the $2,000 charitable donation (a bank record or receipt showing amount and organization).
- Any other deductible items (state taxes paid, mortgage interest statements, property tax, large medical expenses).

A couple of quick recommendations
- Use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) rather than the simplified method — actual appears to give a larger deduction for you.
- If you’re close to the phaseout for student loan interest or have additional deductions that change the math, consider running a quick draft return or using tax software or a preparer to confirm.
- Because you have self‑employment income, consider making estimated quarterly payments next year if you expect similar income, to avoid penalties.

If you want, I can:
- Run a more detailed estimated-tax worksheet for 2024 using any additional numbers you provide (state taxes paid, mortgage interest, other deductions, confirmation of whether $650 is business expense, and whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024).
- Draft a list/email to MOHELA or your clients requesting 1098‑E / 1099‑NEC.

I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. If you want definitive answers or planning (especially about phaseouts and more complex depreciation choices), I recommend consulting a tax professional.

