Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. I’m not a CPA — this is general tax guidance. I’ll also point out a few clarifying questions I need to be precise.

Quick summary (bottom-line)
- Most likely you will take the federal standard deduction (likely larger than your itemized deductions based on the numbers you gave), but confirm any state/local taxes you paid.
- Your contracting income is reported on Schedule C; the contracting-related expenses (including a qualifying home office) reduce that business income.
- Home office: you can use either the simplified method ($5/sq ft) or the actual method (proportion of rent/other home expenses). Based on the numbers you gave, the actual method looks larger, but actual method has more recordkeeping and rules.
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and half SE tax), and possibly Form 8829 (if using actual home-office method). For California, file Form 540 (or the current CA return form) and include the same business income.

Details and calculations using your numbers
Income
- W-2 (TechStart Inc): $84,900
- Contracting income (self-employed): $15,000
- Interest from Chase savings: $920
- Dividends/interest from Charles Schwab: $195
- Total gross income = $84,900 + $15,000 + $920 + $195 = $101,015

Self-employment treatment
- Report the $15,000 contracting on Schedule C.
- Business expenses: if the $650 are business expenses for your contracting work, they are deductible on Schedule C. (If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from being an employee, they are NOT deductible under current law.)
- Home office (see next section) also reduces Schedule C net profit if you meet the requirements.
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net SE income (Schedule SE). You then get an above-the-line deduction for half of the SE tax on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Home office (qualified business use of home)
- Requirements: regular and exclusive use of that space for your business and it must be your principal place of business (or used for meeting clients, etc.). If these are satisfied, you can take a deduction.
- Two methods:
  1) Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For a 100 sq ft office = $500 deduction.
  2) Actual: Multiply percentage of home used for business by home expenses. Your business percentage = 100 ÷ 690 ≈ 14.49%.
     - Rent: $2,400/month → $28,800/year. Home-office portion ≈ 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174.
     - With actual method you’d include a share of rent and potentially utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (and use Form 8829 to compute the deduction).
- Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net business profit (excess may carry forward). For small self-employment income it’s important to check limits.

Student loan interest
- The deductible student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Important: only interest paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible. If the $3,600 you listed are payments made in earlier years (2016–2019), those do not count for 2024. You should get Form 1098-E from your servicer (MOHELA) showing interest paid in 2024.
- There’s an income-based phaseout for this deduction — if your MAGI is over the phaseout range you may get a reduced amount or none.

Charitable donations
- $2,000 to Northwest Harvest: deductible if you itemize. Keep the receipt. If you do not itemize, you cannot deduct it on your federal 1040 (unless a temporary provision applies — generally, for 2024, charities are deductible only if you itemize).
- For most taxpayers with your income mix, the federal standard deduction (single) for 2024 is around $14,600 (confirm exact IRS figure when filing). Compare that to your likely itemized total (charity + state/local tax paid + mortgage interest/property tax if any). Unless you paid significant state taxes or have mortgage interest/property tax, itemizing usually won’t exceed the standard deduction.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- These are not deductible for employees for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended). If the $650 are self-employment/business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job, they’re not deductible.

Which forms to file (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (for above-the-line adjustments such as student loan interest and the deduction for half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 and business expenses (including home office if you deduct it here)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — computes SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this only if doing the actual method for the home-office deduction. Simplified method does not use Form 8829.
- Form 1098-E — student loan servicer should provide if you had qualifying interest payments (use to support student loan interest deduction).
- Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — any you received from clients should be kept and reported (even if you did not get a 1099, you must report the income).

State (California)
- California Form 540 (or the appropriate CA individual return) — report the same income sources; there are differences/adjustments between federal and CA rules, so check the CA Franchise Tax Board guidance or a preparer for specifics. CA generally accepts Schedule C results and home office deductions but has its own conformity quirks.

Other practical items and recommendations
- Gather documents: W-2 from TechStart, any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for the $15k contracting, 1098-E from MOHELA (student loan interest), bank statements showing interest ($920, $195), charity receipt(s), rent statements/ledger, and records showing home square footage.
- Decide home-office method: simplified is easier (no Form 8829, less recordkeeping) and yields $500; actual gives more in your case (~$4,174 from rent alone), but requires more documentation and Form 8829. Using actual method will reduce your net self-employment income (and SE tax).
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, consider quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040-ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Retirement savings options: If you want to lower taxable income, consider SEP-IRA or Solo 401(k) for the self-employment portion — these can reduce AGI and save tax. Also consider whether you’re eligible to deduct a traditional IRA contribution.
- Keep receipts and logs (especially for business expenses and home-office square footage and use).
- If your student-loan interest payments were not made in 2024, they cannot be deducted on the 2024 return — clarify the years those payments were made.

A few clarifying questions so I can be more exact
1. Are the $3,600 of student loan interest payments made in 2024, or are those amounts paid in earlier years (2016–2019)? Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return.
2. Are the $650 expenses related to your contracting work (so they’re business expenses) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job?
3. Do you pay any mortgage interest or property taxes, or did you pay a significant amount of state income tax during the year (e.g., via withholding or estimated payments)? This affects whether itemizing might beat the standard deduction.
4. Do you rent (I assumed yes from the $2,400/mo) or own? (If you own, mortgage interest and property tax change the calculation.)

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers more precisely once you answer the clarifying questions (and I can estimate federal tax liability and show whether standard vs itemized is better).
- Prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer or for use with tax software.
- Draft an email to your tax preparer summarizing your situation and asking for help.

Which of those would you like next?

