Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through this and show the quick math. A few clarifying questions before I run firm numbers or prepare a checklist:
- Was the $15,000 earned as an independent contractor (you received 1099-NEC/1099-MISC) or is that W-2 income from multiple employers?  
- The $3,600 student-loan interest: was that interest paid in 2024 (reported on a 2024 Form 1098-E), or are those earlier-year amounts (2016–2019)? Only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted on the 2024 return.  
- Do you have any state income tax paid, property tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other deductible expenses that you haven’t listed? (SALT is capped at $10,000 for Schedule A.)

Summary answer (short)
- Likely best to take the standard deduction ($14,600 for single in 2024) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state & local tax, mortgage interest, big medical expenses) that push total Schedule A above $14,600.  
- If your $15,000 is self‑employment income, report it on Schedule C and you can deduct business expenses (including the home‑office percentage of your rent and the $650 business expenses). That reduces self‑employment tax and income tax. You’ll also pay self‑employment tax (reported on Schedule SE), but half of that SE tax is an “above the line” adjustment to income.  
- Home office: you qualify as self‑employed (if you are) — actual-expense method (your allocated share of rent) is more favorable than the simplified method in your case. Use Form 8829 (actual method) or the simplified $5/sqft method if you prefer simplicity.  
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible (adjustment to income) if you paid it in 2024 and your income isn’t too high; there are AGI phaseouts. If your $3,600 was paid earlier than 2024, you cannot deduct it on the 2024 return.  
- Charitable $2,000: deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, you do not get that deduction. Keep the receipt.

Detailed walk-through and calculations (using the numbers you provided)
1) Income you reported
- W-2 wages: $84,900  
- Self‑employment/contracting gross: $15,000  
- Interest (Chase + Schwab): $920 + $195 = $1,115  
Total gross income = $101,015

2) Home office deduction (you said 100 sq ft exclusive; rental = $2,400/mo; total home = 690 sq ft)
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800  
- Business-use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%  
- Allocated rent for home office (actual-expense method) ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176 for the year.  
- Simplified method would be $5 × 100 sq ft = $500. Because $4,176 > $500, the actual-expense method yields a larger deduction (assuming you only have rent as the allocable home expense you want to use — you could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. and increase the actual-method deduction).

3) Self-employment profit estimate and SE tax (if $15,000 is contractor income)
- Gross Schedule C income: $15,000  
- Less home office (actual) ≈ $4,176 and other business expenses $650  
- Estimated net profit ≈ $15,000 − $4,176 − $650 = $10,174  
- SE tax is charged on 92.35% of net profit: 0.9235 × $10,174 ≈ $9,394  
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $9,394 ≈ $1,437 (approx).  
- You can deduct one-half of SE tax as an adjustment to income: ≈ $718.50.

4) Student loan interest
- Maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (an adjustment to income), subject to income phaseouts and only for interest paid in the tax year and reported on Form 1098‑E. Clarify whether your $3,600 was paid in 2024 — if yes you can generally deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseout). If the payments were in earlier years, they are not deductible on the 2024 return.

5) Charitable donation
- $2,000 cash gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $650
- If these are business expenses of your contracting work, they are deductible on Schedule C (we included them above). If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were a W‑2 employee and your employer didn’t reimburse), they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under the current tax law.

7) Itemize vs standard (quick comparison)
- Your likely itemizable items from what you listed: charitable $2,000. (You might have state income tax withheld or paid — not provided.)  
- The 2024 standard deduction for single = $14,600.  
- Unless you have additional deductible amounts (state/local taxes up to $10k, mortgage interest, big medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, or other large items), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. The home office deduction does NOT go on Schedule A — it reduces your self‑employment income on Schedule C/8829.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return  
- Schedule C — profit/loss from business (report your $15k and business expenses)  
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax  
- Form 8829 — expenses for business use of your home (if you use the actual-expense method). If you use the simplified home‑office method, you do not file 8829 (enter the simplified-home-office amount on Schedule C).  
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments like deductible student loan interest (if applicable) — this flows to Form 1040.  
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable donation would go here)  
- Keep 1099‑NEC (contracting), W‑2 (TechStart), 1099‑INT (Chase, Schwab), 1098‑E (student loan interest, if issued for 2024).  
- California: file CA Form 540 (or 540NR if nonresident). California rules differ in places; bring your federal details to your CA return — CA does not allow the federal deduction for state/local taxes in the same way, and there are other adjustments.

Practical next steps I recommend
1. Confirm whether $15,000 is self-employment (contractor) income. If so: track and categorize all business expenses, use Schedule C, pay estimated taxes (or make sure you’ve paid quarterly estimated taxes to cover SE tax and income tax).  
2. Confirm whether the $3,600 student loan interest was paid in 2024 and if you received Form 1098‑E for 2024. If yes, you can likely deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income limits). If no 1098‑E or if payments were in prior years, it won’t be deductible on your 2024 return.  
3. Gather supporting docs: receipts for the $2,000 charity, 1099s, W‑2, documentation of home-office size and how it’s used, rent payment records, utility/bill amounts if using actual home‑office calculation, invoices and receipts for the $650 business expenses.  
4. If self‑employed, estimate federal and CA state taxes and, if necessary, make/adjust 2025 estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

Would you like me to:
- Run a more precise tax estimate (federal tax owed and effective marginal rates) using the assumptions above? (I’ll need confirmation about whether the $15k is 1099 income and whether the $3,600 interest was paid in 2024.)  
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect for filing or for a CPA?

If you confirm the contractor status and the student‑loan timing, I’ll compute a full estimated federal tax (and estimated quarterly payment recommendation).

