Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — that’s enough to give a useful, practical answer, but I do need two quick clarifying points before finalizing numbers and recommendations:

1. Is the $15,000 from your IT contracting reported to you on 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (i.e., self‑employment income), or was it paid as W‑2 wages?  
2. The $3,600 student‑loan interest you listed — was that interest you actually paid in tax year 2024 (and do you have a 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2024)? If it’s interest paid in earlier years, it is not deductible on your 2024 return.

I’ll proceed below with clear assumptions. If you confirm or correct the two items above I’ll update the numbers.

Summary (assumptions)
- Assumed: $15,000 is self‑employment (1099) income.  
- Assumed: $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 and you have Form 1098‑E (I’ll show the effect if it’s not).  
- You live in CA (state filing also required); I cover federal guidance and list CA form you’ll need.

What you can deduct and how to treat each item

1) W‑2 wages ($84,900)
- Report on Form 1040, line for wages (from your W‑2). No special treatment.

2) Contracting income ($15,000)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Business gross = $15,000.
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see home‑office and the $650 below).
- Subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

3) Interest income ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115)
- Report on Form 1040 as interest income. (Schedule B is only required if total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 — you’re under that, so no Schedule B required.)

4) Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/mo = $28,800/year)
- Because you are self‑employed, you can take the home‑office deduction for the business portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 (flat, easy).  
  b) Regular method: allocate actual expenses. For rent: 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176. You may also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. The regular method almost certainly gives a larger deduction here.
- If you use the regular method, report it with Schedule C and Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). Form 8829 also limits the deduction to net profit from that business; any excess home‑office deduction carries forward.

5) Student‑loan interest ($3,600)
- The federal student loan interest deduction allows up to $2,500 of qualified student‑loan interest as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), subject to modified AGI phaseouts. You must have actually paid interest in 2024 and receive Form 1098‑E. If your interest payments were in prior years (2016–2019), those are not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Whether you can take the full $2,500 depends on your (modified) AGI; if your AGI is above the phaseout range you may get a reduced amount or none. If you want, I can estimate whether you’re within the phaseout once you confirm the actual 2024 interest paid.

6) Charitable gift ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest)
- If you itemize, this is deductible on Schedule A (must have receipt). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this (except limited special rules which generally do not apply to 2024).
- Keep the written receipt.

7) $650 unreimbursed expenses
- If these were expenses related to your self‑employment work (equipment, supplies, mileage reimbursed, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee for that work), those are not deductible on federal returns for tax years through 2025 (TCJA suspension), so they would not be deductible federally.

Standard deduction vs. itemize (federal)
- 2024 estimated standard deduction for a single filer: $14,600.
- Typical itemizable items you’ve listed for 2024: charitable $2,000. Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment, not an itemized deduction, so it does not count toward Schedule A comparison. Unless you have additional itemizable amounts (state income/property taxes, mortgage interest, big medical expenses above the threshold, large charitable donations, etc.), your itemized total looks far below the standard deduction.
- Conclusion (based on provided items): You will likely take the standard deduction. Itemize only if your total Schedule A deductions (state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, mortgage interest, property taxes, unreimbursed medical above threshold, larger charitable gifts, etc.) exceed $14,600.

Estimated illustrative federal calculation (with assumptions above)
- Gross income: 84,900 (W‑2) + 15,000 (self‑empl) + 1,115 (interest) = $101,015.
- Schedule C allowed business expenses (example):
  - Home office (regular method, alloc. of rent): ≈ $4,176
  - Other business expenses: $650
  Net self‑employment income ≈ 15,000 − 4,826 = $10,174.
- Self‑employment tax: SE applies to 92.35% × $10,174 ≈ $9,398 → SE tax ≈ $1,437. Half of that (~$718) is an adjustment reducing AGI.
- AGI before student loan deduction ≈ 84,900 + 10,174 + 1,115 − 718 = $95,471.
- Student loan interest deduction (max $2,500) would reduce AGI further if eligible; but note the $3,600 you quoted exceeds the $2,500 federal cap for the deduction.
- After the standard deduction of $14,600 (if you take it), estimated taxable income would be in the ballpark of $78k (depends on final AGI adjustments). This is a rough estimate — I can run a more precise calculation if you confirm the two clarifying items and any other deductible items (state tax withheld, property taxes, mortgage interest, IRA or SEP contributions, etc.).

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments: e.g., student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit/Loss from Business) — reports the $15,000 contracting and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 if you use the regular method for home office
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (charitable donations, SALT up to $10k, etc.)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC documents for contracting income (if received)
- 1099‑INT for bank/broker interest (Chase, Schwab)
- 1098‑E for student loan interest (if you have one for 2024)

California state
- California resident return: Form 540 (or 540 2EZ, depending). California rules differ from the federal code in some areas (and some deductions/credits differ). Keep all state‑tax withholding info from your W‑2 and any California estimated payments. If you want, I can outline CA differences once you confirm more details.

Other practical items & recommendations
- Home‑office method: Regular method yields a much larger deduction for your numbers (≈$4,176 vs $500 simplified). Use Form 8829 and keep rent and utility records.
- Student‑loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year is deductible (max $2,500). Confirm you have a 1098‑E for 2024.
- Keep receipts for charitable gifts and business expenses.
- Consider retirement‑account contributions (SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k)) to lower taxable income for the self‑employed part — could reduce both income and SE tax.
- Because you have self‑employment income, think about estimated quarterly tax payments for 2025 (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If unsure or your situation is more complex (e.g., multiple state filings, carryforwards, etc.), consult a tax pro — I can help assemble the numbers for them.

Next steps I can do for you
- Recalculate federal taxable income and tax owed with the two clarifications (is the $15k 1099/self‑employment; confirm whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024).  
- Prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing (W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, receipts, etc.).  
- Draft a summary you can give to a tax preparer or upload to tax software.

Tell me the two clarifying answers (1099 vs W‑2 for the $15k; and did you pay $3,600 in student‑loan interest in 2024 and have a 1098‑E). I’ll update the exact numbers and finalize which forms/deductions to claim.

