Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I’ll walk through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal and California forms you’ll likely need. I’ll make a couple of clear assumptions; if any assumption is wrong please tell me and I’ll recalc.

Assumptions I’m using
- Your W-2 wage = $84,900 (TechStart).
- Self-employment (IT contracting) gross = $15,000 (3 clients).
- Interest income = $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115.
- The $650 of “unreimbursed expenses” are expenses of your contracting work (if they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible for federal tax after TCJA).
- Rent = $2,400/month (you are a renter), home size = 690 sq ft, home office exclusive space = 100 sq ft.
- Student loan interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for (you listed $3,600). I’ll note the deduction limit/phaseout below.
- No mortgage interest or property tax (you’re renting) unless you tell me otherwise.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self-employment (business) expenses — deductible on Schedule C.
  - The $650 (if business-related) is deductible on Schedule C.
  - Home-office allocation (see below) — deductible on Schedule C as a business expense if the home office is used for the self-employed contracting activity and meets the regular/exclusive-use test.
- Home office:
  - Two methods: simplified ($5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft) or actual-expense method.
  - Simplified: 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction.
  - Actual method: allocate a portion of rent and eligible home expenses to the business: business % = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%.
    - Rent portion = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174. You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable.
  - For your numbers the actual method (rent allocation ≈ $4,174) will very likely give you a larger deduction than the simplified $500. Use actual method if you have records of rent and utilities.
  - Home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income (and therefore income and self-employment tax); it is NOT taken on Schedule A.
- Self-employment tax:
  - You’ll owe self-employment tax on net self-employment income (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on your Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest:
  - Federal maximum deduction is $2,500 (even if you paid $3,600). There is a MAGI phaseout for single filers — if your MAGI is above the phaseout range you cannot take it (or you get a reduced amount).
  - Based on the rough income estimate below, you may be above the phaseout and may not be able to claim all/any of the student-loan interest deduction; see “MAGI/phaseout” notes below.
- Charitable contributions:
  - Your $2,000 gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct it on the federal return.
- Other deductions:
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job are not deductible on your federal return (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions through 2025).
  - State and local taxes (SALT) — deductible only if you itemize and capped at $10,000 total (state income tax + property tax + real estate tax).

2) Quick approximate calculations (to show effects)
- Starting point (after treating $650 as business expense and using the actual home-office allocation):
  - W-2 wages: $84,900
  - Net self-employment (estimate): gross $15,000 − business expenses:
    - $650 other business expense
    - home-office rent allocation ≈ $4,174
    - Net SE income ≈ $15,000 − $4,824 = $10,176
  - Interest income: $1,115
  - Total income before SE tax adjustment = 84,900 + 10,176 + 1,115 = $96,191
  - Self-employment tax (approx): net SE × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $1,438
  - Half of SE tax deductible ≈ $719 (an above-the-line adjustment)
  - AGI before student loan interest ≈ $96,191 − $719 ≈ $95,472
  - Student loan interest: federal max $2,500 (you paid $3,600). Whether you can claim any depends on MAGI phaseout (see next section).
  - Standard deduction (single, 2024): $14,600 (if you take standard).
  - Itemized deductions likely low unless you have large state tax or mortgage interest — your $2,000 charitable gift alone is far below the standard deduction.
- If you did NOT take the home-office actual method (only the simplified $500) your net SE income would be larger and AGI would be higher — that makes the student loan interest deduction even less likely to be allowed.

3) Student loan interest — MAGI/phaseout (important)
- The student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out by MAGI. The phaseout thresholds change each year (inflation adjustment).
- With the AGI estimate above (~$95k before student-loan adjustment), you are likely at or above the phaseout range used in recent years for single filers, so you may be ineligible (or only eligible for a reduced amount). If you want, I can calculate the precise phaseout amount and your exact allowable deduction if you give me any other adjustments to AGI, or if you want me to assume you claim the full $2,500.
- Note: only interest paid in the tax year and on a qualified student loan is eligible.

4) Itemize vs. standard deduction (federal)
- Standard deduction for single (2024) = $14,600.
- Your readily available itemizable deduction here is $2,000 charitable gift. Unless you have significant deductible state income taxes, property taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts that push total Schedule A over $14,600, you will take the standard deduction.
- Likely recommendation: take the standard deduction on your federal return, and claim the home-office and business expenses on Schedule C (they reduce taxable income without requiring itemizing). The home-office deduction does not require itemizing.

5) How the home office should be treated
- If the 100 sq ft space is used exclusively and regularly for your contracting work, claim it on Schedule C as a business expense.
- Choose actual-expense method vs simplified:
  - Actual-expense method: allocate % of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to business. For you, rent allocation (~$4,174) > simplified ($500), so actual method will likely be better.
  - The allocated home-office deduction reduces your net Schedule C income, which lowers both income tax and self-employment tax.
- Documentation: keep a floor plan, calculation of square footage, rent receipts/lease, utility bills, and records showing exclusive business use.

6) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report your contracting income and business expenses (including home-office allocation or simplified home-office deduction).
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — to report certain adjustments (self-employment adjustments like half SE tax and student loan interest if allowed; Schedule C profit flows to 1040 via Schedule 1).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely you will not, but if you do, charitable gifts go here).
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — report any 1099s you received for contracting. Even if you did not receive 1099s, you still report the income.
- Form 1099-INTs — interest from Chase and Schwab (you’ll receive 1099-INTs for those amounts).
- If you pay estimated taxes: Form 1040-ES (for quarterly payments) during the year; otherwise reconcile at filing.
- State: California Form 540 (resident return). Note that California has its own rules and may not follow all federal adjustments exactly.

7) California (state) considerations
- File CA Form 540. California generally requires reporting your federal AGI as a starting point but has differences (some federal adjustments may be added back or disallowed). State treatment of student loan interest and other adjustments can differ from federal — check Form 540 instructions or software.
- If you live and work in San Diego, you still claim the home-office deduction on federal Schedule C; California generally follows federal treatment for Schedule C business income, but verify any state-specific differences.

8) Other practical items / next steps
- If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are actually employee expenses (for your W-2 job), they are not deductible federally — clarify source of those expenses.
- Keep receipts: rent, utilities, charitable receipt, 1099s, 1098-E or loan statements for student loan interest, and records for the home office.
- If you haven’t paid estimated taxes on the contracting income during the year, you may owe underpayment penalties; consider paying estimated taxes going forward.
- If you want, I can:
  - Recompute exact AGI and taxable income using either the actual home-office method or simplified method and then show estimated federal tax and self-employment tax.
  - Check whether the student loan interest deduction phases out for your exact AGI (I’ll need to know other adjustments or confirm you want me to use the estimates above).
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to gather before filing or prepare a draft email for an accountant.

Bottom line / recommendation
- Use Schedule C and Schedule SE to report your contracting income.
- Claim the home-office deduction using the actual-expense method (allocate rent/utility share) — it’s almost certainly better than the simplified method for your numbers.
- You will probably take the federal standard deduction ($14,600 for single 2024) rather than itemizing, unless you have substantial additional itemizable amounts (large state income tax or mortgage interest).
- Student loan interest may be limited or phased out at your income level — check the MAGI phaseout using your full AGI; you may not be able to claim the full $2,500.
- File CA Form 540 for state taxes and verify state-specific conformity for the deductions above.

If you want, I can:
- Recompute everything precisely (GAI, taxable income, estimate of federal tax & self-employment tax) using either actual home-office allocation (I used rent only) or simplified method.
- Or, if you prefer, I can prepare a short checklist of documents and amounts to send to a tax preparer. Which would you like next?

