--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 8094 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Likely take the standard deduction for 2024 (single: $14,600) because your known itemizable expenses (charity $2,000, no mortgage interest listed, unknown state taxes) are probably less than the standard amount.  
- Your self‑employment (contracting) income and related expenses — including a home office deduction — are reported on Schedule C and reduce your taxable/self‑employment income whether you itemize or not.  
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts) and does NOT affect the itemize vs standard decision.  
- Unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses are generally NOT deductible (post‑TCJA), but unreimbursed expenses that relate to your self‑employment are deductible on Schedule C.

Details and what to do next

1) What you can deduct (overview)
- Self‑employment business expenses (report on Schedule C):
  - Your home office deduction (if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the contracting business).
  - The $650 of unreimbursed expenses, but only if those expenses are business expenses related to the contracting work. If they are unreimbursed W‑2 employee costs, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 in most cases.
  - Any other ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, software, mileage, phone portion, subcontractor costs, etc.).
- Self‑employment tax deduction:
  - You must pay self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). One half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income (above the line).
- Student loan interest:
  - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (phased out at higher MAGI). You need your Form 1098‑E from MOHELA (or your record of interest paid).
  - Only interest paid in the tax year counts — interest paid in prior years is not deductible on this year’s return.
- Charitable contributions:
  - $2,000 cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also claim these on Schedule A.
- Investment income:
  - $920 interest and $195 in taxable dividends are reported on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or if you have certain foreign accounts).

2) Home office rules (how to treat it)
- Requirements:
  - Regular and exclusive use of the space for your trade or business, and used as your principal place of business (or for meeting clients, etc.). Based on what you said (100 sq ft exclusive), that sounds like it can qualify if it’s used only for the contracting work.
- Methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → for 100 sq ft that’s $500.
  - Actual expense method: prorate actual home expenses by the business percentage. Your business percentage = 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%. If you pay rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year, the rent portion deductible for the home office = 28,800 * 14.49% ≈ $4,170 (plus a proportional share of utilities, renters insurance, etc.). The actual method yields a much larger deduction here.
- Filing:
  - If you use the actual method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and include the result on Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you report the allowed deduction on Schedule C but do not file Form 8829.
- Important limits:
  - The home office deduction reduces net profit on Schedule C. If it creates or increases a loss, some deductions may be limited (and excess may carry forward). Also the deduction cannot exceed your gross business income from the business before the home office and self‑employment tax impacts.

3) Itemize vs. standard
- Itemizable items you mentioned: charitable $2,000. You haven’t listed mortgage interest or sizable state/local tax payments; with only $2,000 in known itemizable deductions, the 2024 standard deduction for a single filer ($14,600) is almost certainly larger.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction and still claim business deductions (Schedule C) and above‑the‑line student loan interest. Itemize only if your total Schedule A deductions (charity + SALT up to $10k + mortgage interest + medical over threshold, etc.) exceed $14,600.

4) Forms you’ll need
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest (this can vary by year; the deduction flows into Form 1040).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for the home office (not needed for simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize deductions.
- Schedule B — only if your interest and dividend totals exceed reporting thresholds or you have other reportable items.
- Keep: W‑2 from TechStart; any 1099‑NEC or records from your three clients; Form 1098‑E for student loan interest; receipts for charitable gifts; records/receipts for business expenses and home costs (rent, utilities, insurance).

5) Other items to watch
- Estimated tax payments: Because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) during the year to avoid underpayment penalties if withholding on your W‑2 doesn’t cover your total tax liability.
- Self‑employment tax: expect ~15.3% on net self‑employment earnings (you get to deduct half of that when computing AGI).
- Keep receipts and accurate logs (home office square footage, dates, business‑use percentage, mileage logs if claiming vehicle use, receipts for business expenses).
- If you received no 1099‑NEC for some of the contracting income, you still must report the income.

6) Quick illustrative estimate (rough, with assumptions)
(Assumptions: student loan interest deductible up to $2,500; the $650 and home office relate to contracting; rent =$2,400/mo; simplified tax bracket logic.)
- Combined gross: W‑2 $84,900 + contracting $15,000 + interest $920 + dividends $195 ≈ $101,015.
- If you take the actual home office portion of rent (~$4,170) + $650 other business expenses ⇒ net contracting profit ≈ $10,176.
- Self‑employment tax on that profit ≈ $1,400–$1,500; half (~$700–$750) is deductible above the line.
- Subtract student loan interest (up to $2,500) and half SE tax from AGI, then apply the standard deduction $14,600. With these assumptions you’d almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction than itemizing.
Note: this is a rough example — exact tax depends on exact figures, any other deductions/withholding, and phaseouts.

7) Questions I need to finish this and/or to provide a more exact estimate
- Is the $3,600 student‑loan interest amount paid in 2024 (or are those years you listed when the loan existed)? Do you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2024? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.)
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employment (contracting) or to W‑2 employment?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC forms from your 3 clients, or did they pay you via other methods?
- Is the $2,400/month a rent payment (not mortgage)?
- Do you have any significant state/local taxes or mortgage interest that you haven’t listed?

If you want, I can:
- Walk through a more exact tax estimate if you confirm the questions above and provide any missing numbers (withholdings on W‑2, state tax paid, 1099 amounts, exact interest paid in 2024).
- Draft a checklist or a step‑by‑step worksheet you can use to collect all the documents needed for filing or to give to a preparer.

Disclaimer: I’m not a CPA or attorney. This is general information based on 2024 U.S. federal tax rules; for binding tax advice tailored to your situation, consult a qualified tax professional.

