Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical summary of what you can (and probably can’t) deduct, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, what federal and state forms you’ll likely need, and next steps. I have a few quick clarifying questions at the end so I can give more precise estimates or run numbers for you.

1) What looks deductible (federal)
- W‑2 wages (TechStart Inc., $84,900): reported on Form 1040 (you don’t “deduct” them; they’re income).
- Self‑employment (IT contracting, $15,000): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (equipment, supplies, phone/internet portion, mileage, fees, accounting, etc.).
  - The $650 unreimbursed expenses: if these were business expenses related to your contracting work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If they were expenses as a W‑2 employee, they are not deductible on the federal return (employee miscellaneous itemized deductions are suspended through 2025).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 total taxable interest. Report on Form 1040 (Schedule B is only required if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500).
- Charitable gift: $2,000 to Northwest Harvest with receipt — deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep the receipt (organization name, date, amount).
- Student‑loan interest: you can only deduct student loan interest that you actually paid in the tax year. The maximum federal student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). Important: clarify whether the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 or a historical total — you can only deduct interest paid in 2024 on your 2024 return. Also, because the deduction phases out for higher MAGI (single filers start phasing out at a relatively modest AGI and fully phase out above a cap), your eligibility depends on your MAGI after adjustments.
- Home office: deductible only for self‑employed/independent contractor use (see details below). Not available for W‑2-only employees.

2) Home office treatment (you said 100 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent = $2,400/month)
You appear eligible to claim a home office deduction for the portion of your contracting business that is run from that exclusive 100 sq ft space — but only if the contracting income is reported as self‑employment (Schedule C) and the home office is used regularly and exclusively for that trade/business.

Two methods:

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
  - For 100 sq ft → deduction = 100 × $5 = $500 (simple, less recordkeeping).

- Regular method (more recordkeeping; often larger when rent is high):
  - Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Deductible portion of rent = 14.49% × $28,800 annual rent = ≈ $4,171.
  - You would also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), etc., and report totals on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach to Schedule C. Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business for the year; any excess may carry forward under certain rules.

Quick comparison: Simplified = $500 vs regular rent allocation ≈ $4,171 (plus any utilities). The regular method will likely give a bigger deduction in your case because rent is high, but it requires tracking and may reduce net profit (and therefore affect self‑employment tax and other computation limitations). Also if net profit is small, the home office deduction could be limited.

3) Self‑employment tax and other effects
- Net profit from Schedule C (contracting income minus business expenses including home office) is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% before the deduction for half of SE tax). You’ll calculate SE tax on Schedule SE and you can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) on qualified business income from your contracting, subject to rules and income limits. That can reduce taxable income but is more complex.

4) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- You only benefit from itemizing if your total itemized deductions on Schedule A exceed the standard deduction for a single filer. Your listed potential itemized items right now:
  - Charitable gifts = $2,000
  - Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (above-the-line), not an itemized deduction (so it doesn’t help decide itemize vs standard).
  - You did not list mortgage interest, large state/local taxes (SALT), or medical expenses above the AGI threshold — these often drive itemizing decisions.
- Given only $2,000 in charitable donations and no mortgage interest listed, it’s very likely the standard deduction will be more beneficial for federal taxes. (If you have additional itemizable amounts — large CA state/local tax payments, mortgage interest, unreimbursed casualty etc. — bring those into the calculation.)
- For California state taxes: CA has its own rules and standard deduction. You’ll report all income on your California return and then apply state deductions/credits. If you have large state-level deductions, that affects CA but not the federal standard deduction decision.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (if you have additional income or adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your $15,000 contracting income and related business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only if interest + dividends > $1,500 (your total interest = $1,115 so not required)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for QBI deduction if you are eligible (many self‑employed filers will want to check this)
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC records from clients (or other proof of income)
- 1099‑INT or year‑end statements for interest (bank/brokerage)
- Form 1098‑E (if issued) for student loan interest — or documentation from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024

For California:
- CA Form 540 (resident return) and any related schedules. (State specifics vary; California has its own rules for itemized deductions and conformity differences.)

6) Recordkeeping — what to gather now
- W‑2 from TechStart Inc.
- 1099‑NEC / invoices or bank records showing $15,000 contracting income
- Bank/Schwab statements or 1099‑INT(s) showing the $1,115 interest
- Charity receipt for $2,000 (date, organization EIN or name)
- Documentation of student loan interest paid in 2024 (statements from MOHELA, Form 1098‑E if provided)
- Lease agreement and proof of rent paid for the year (ledgers, canceled checks, bank statements)
- Records for utilities, internet, renter’s insurance if you plan to use regular home office method
- Records/detail for the $650 unreimbursed expenses (what they were and how they relate to the contracting work)

7) Likely outcome / recommendation (based on the information you gave)
- For federal purposes, you will very likely take the standard deduction because your itemizable amounts appear small (charity $2,000 only). If you take the standard deduction, your home office and other business expenses still reduce your self‑employment income (they are claimed on Schedule C) — so the home office deduction is worthwhile if you are self‑employed.
- Use the regular home office method if you want the larger deduction and can document rent/utilities; simplified method is easier and may be chosen for convenience.
- Make sure student loan interest amount is for 2024 and check MAGI phaseout — you may or may not be eligible for that deduction given your income.
- Expect to file: 1040 + Schedule C + Schedule SE, and possibly Form 8829 and Form 8995 (QBI).

8) Quick math example (illustrative)
- Gross income: 84,900 (W‑2) + 15,000 (contracting) + 1,115 (interest) = 101,015.
- If you claim the regular home office allocable rent ≈ $4,171 and the $650 business expenses on Schedule C:
  - Schedule C gross 15,000 − (4,171 + 650 + any other business expenses) = net self‑employment income (example ≈ 10,179 before other expenses).
  - You’ll pay self‑employment tax on that net and be eligible to deduct half of the SE tax on Form 1040.
- Charitable $2,000 only helps if itemizing — likely less than standard deduction, so standard deduction is probably better.

9) Questions for you (so I can run exact numbers and give final recommendations)
- Is the $15,000 contracting income reported to you as 1099‑NEC (i.e., are you an independent contractor), or were you a W‑2 temporary employee for those gigs?
- Is the $650 in unreimbursed expenses related to the contracting work (and paid in 2024)?
- Is the $3,600 student‑loan interest the amount you paid in 2024, or is that a historical total from 2016–2019? Do you have a 1098‑E for 2024?
- Do you want me to: a) estimate your federal tax liability and self‑employment tax with the numbers you provided, or b) prepare a “choose standard vs itemize” worksheet showing the comparison? If yes, I can compute both simplified and regular home‑office scenarios and show the results.

10) Final notes / recommended next steps
- Confirm the clarifying items above (especially the student loan interest timing and whether the $650 is business related).
- If you expect to owe more than $1,000 in tax after withholding, consider whether you should have made estimated quarterly payments for the self‑employment income (Form 1040‑ES) — you may face penalties if you didn’t; we can estimate any underpayment.
- If you’d like, I can run a detailed, line‑by‑line estimate of your 2024 federal return (including SE tax and estimating QBI) and show whether regular or simplified home office yields a better result.

Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions (especially whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 and whether the $650 is business-related) and whether you want a detailed tax estimate — I’ll calculate the numbers for you.

