Thanks — that’s a good set of details. Below I’ll cover (1) what’s deductible, (2) how to treat the home‑office, (3) whether you should likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) the federal forms you’ll need, and (5) recommended next steps & questions I need to give a precise calculation.

1) What you can (generally) deduct
- W-2 wages ($84,900): reported on Form 1040 (no deduction required).
- Self-employment (contracting) income ($15,000): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see home‑office below and the $650). Net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Interest (Chase $920, Schwab $195 = $1,115): taxable interest. If total interest/dividends < $1,500 and you don’t meet other Schedule B triggers, you usually report them directly on Form 1040 (watch for 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).
- Student loan interest ($3,600): the student‑loan interest deduction is an “adjustment to income” for interest you actually paid in the tax year (not interest paid in earlier years). The maximum annual deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out based on your modified adjusted gross income). You need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer to substantiate. Clarify whether $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 or earlier years — only 2024 payments matter for 2024 return.
- Charitable donation ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. You have the receipt — good. For cash donations, keep bank/acknowledgment records.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650): whether deductible depends on whether these were incurred as a self‑employed contractor or as an employee:
  - If they are business expenses related to your contracting work, include them on Schedule C (they reduce self‑employment profit).
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on federal returns under the current tax law (post‑TCJA).

2) Home‑office treatment (renting, 100 sq ft exclusive, home is 690 sq ft, rent $2,400/month)
You can choose either the simplified method or the actual method for a home office used by a sole proprietor/independent contractor.

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - No Form 8829 needed. Simpler recordkeeping.

- Actual method (you appear to benefit more from this method with the numbers you gave):
  - Percentage of home used for business = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
  - Business portion of rent = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176.
  - If you have utilities or renter’s insurance that can be allocated, those business portions would also be deductible (you didn’t list utilities; include them if applicable).
  - To use actual method for a home office when self‑employed, you typically file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the allowable deduction to Schedule C.
  - Note: the home office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. Excess carryover rules apply only for depreciation component under some circumstances.

Recommendation: With your rent and 100 sq ft, the actual method (≈$4,176 from rent alone) will almost certainly beat the simplified $500 method, assuming you have at least $4,176 of net profit in that business and no limiting rule applies. Use the actual method and track utility/renter’s insurance amounts to increase the deduction if you have them.

3) Self‑employment tax, estimated taxes, and other interactions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare, ~15.3% on roughly 92.35% of net income). You then get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- Because you have contracting income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- The home‑office deduction and the $650 of business expenses reduce Schedule C net profit and thus reduce self‑employment tax and income tax.

4) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- You can either itemize on Schedule A or take the standard deduction (not both).
- Itemizable items you listed: charitable $2,000. You may also have SALT (state and local taxes) up to the $10,000 federal cap — you didn’t give the amount withheld or paid, so I can’t include it in a comparison. Mortgage interest would also matter (you didn’t list any).
- Given the info you provided (no mortgage interest listed, charitable $2,000, likely SALT maybe below the standard deduction), many taxpayers in your situation find the standard deduction is larger than their itemized total. It’s likely (but not certain) that you’ll be better off taking the standard deduction — however if your state/local tax + charitable + other itemizable items exceed the standard deduction, then itemize.
- If you want, I can run a quick compare using the exact 2024 standard deduction amount and an estimate of your state tax paid (you can provide the year‑end state tax withheld or estimate) to determine which is better.

5) Federal forms you will commonly use
- Form 1040 (Individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (if you claim student‑loan interest adjustment or other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your contracting income and business expenses, including home‑office deduction entries from Form 8829 if using the actual method.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you elect the actual method. Not needed for the simplified method.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax; half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize.
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend thresholds or special circumstances apply (total taxable interest and ordinary dividends over $1,500, or having certain foreign accounts).
- Keep an eye out for: W‑2 from TechStart, 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC for contracting clients (or you may need to report cash/transactions even without a 1099), 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from Chase and Schwab, and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA.

6) Recordkeeping & receipts to keep
- W‑2, 1099s, brokerage year‑end statements.
- 1098‑E for student loan interest.
- Charitable donation receipt/acknowledgement for the $2,000 donation.
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills (for actual home‑office allocation).
- Receipts/invoices for the $650 business expenses and any other Schedule C expenses.

7) Items that need clarification from you (so I can give exact numbers)
- Did you actually pay $3,600 in student loan interest in 2024, or is that interest paid in earlier years? Only 2024 interest paid is relevant to your 2024 return.
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your contracting work (self‑employment) or to your W‑2 job?
- Do you have utility amounts for the home (electric, internet, renter’s insurance) that you’d like to include in the home‑office actual method calculation?
- How much state tax was withheld on your W‑2 (or paid during the year)? That matters for the itemize vs. standard decision because of the SALT cap.
- Do you receive any 1099‑NEC forms from the three clients? If not, do you have records (invoices/payments) of the $15,000 contracting income?

8) Quick rough example (illustrative)
Assuming:
- Contracting gross $15,000.
- Business expenses: home office actual rent portion ≈ $4,176 + $650 other business expenses = $4,826.
- Net Schedule C ≈ $15,000 – $4,826 = $10,174.
- Self‑employment taxable amount ≈ 10,174 × 0.9235 ≈ 9,399; SE tax ≈ 9,399 × 15.3% ≈ $1,438; half deductible ≈ $719.
- Total income before adjustments ~ W‑2 & interest (84,900 + 1,115 = 86,015) + Schedule C net 10,174 = 96,189.
- Subtract half SE tax ≈ 719 -> AGI ≈ 95,470. Subtract student loan interest (up to $2,500 if eligible) and the standard deduction to get taxable income.
This is only an illustrative walk‑through; I can do an exact computation once you confirm the clarifying points above and whether you want me to use the actual standard deduction number for 2024.

9) State (California) notes
- California returns have differences from federal (some adjustments and different treatment of certain deductions). CA has its own standard deduction and rules; a CA home‑office deduction generally follows similar principles but confirm when preparing CA Form 540. If you want, I can also show CA filing items once you confirm state withholding or other items.

