Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Summary up front
- Take the standard deduction (most likely). Your likely itemized deductions (charity $2,000, no mortgage interest shown, no SALT beyond $10k cap) are far less than the 2024 standard deduction for a single filer.
- Your contracting income is self‑employment income — report it on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and deduct business expenses (including a home‑office allocation) on Schedule C.
- Home office: because you’re self‑employed you can claim a deduction for the exclusive 100 sq ft office. The actual‑expense method (a share of rent) will be larger than the simplified method here.
- Student loan interest: the federal cap is $2,500/year and it phases out at higher MAGI; with your wages + self‑employment income you may be above the phaseout and may not get the deduction. Check your MAGI carefully.
- Interest from Chase/Schwab ($1,115) is reported as interest income on Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INTs).
- You’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and Schedule A only if you itemize.

Detailed breakdown and examples

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $84,900 (TechStart — report on Form 1040 with W‑2).
- Self‑employment (3 IT clients): $15,000 — report on Schedule C (even if you received 1099s or not, this income must be reported).
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — reported on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if interest > $1,500; you’re under that).

2) Business expenses (what you can deduct against the $15,000)
- Home office: You say 100 sq ft exclusive out of a 690 sq ft home -> percentage = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Rent: $2,400/mo → $28,800/year. Actual‑expense home office allocation = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176.
  - Simplified method would be $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
  - Use whichever method gives the larger deduction (actual here ≈ $4,176 > $500). Keep records (floor plan, receipts, lease).
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650): If these were business expenses for your contracting work (supplies, travel, phone, software, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed expenses as a W‑2 employee, they are NOT deductible (suspended through 2025).
- Other allowable business expenses: any client‑related costs, software, mileage (standard mileage rate), professional subscriptions, home Internet portion, etc., if ordinary and necessary for the contracting work.

3) Self‑employment tax and adjustment for AGI
- After Schedule C net profit is computed, you pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) — roughly 15.3% on 92.35% of net SE income.
- You get to deduct one‑half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces your AGI).

Quick illustrative math (estimates)
- Self‑employment gross: $15,000
- Less home office (actual) ≈ $4,176 and business expenses $650 → estimated net SE income ≈ $10,174.
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × $10,174 ≈ $1,439; half deductible ≈ $719.
- Combined gross income ≈ $84,900 + $1,115 + $10,174 = $96,189.
- Subtract half SE tax (~$719) → AGI before student‑loan adjustment ≈ $95,470.
- Student loan interest deduction: maximum allowed is $2,500, but it phases out at higher MAGI. With an AGI in the mid‑$90k range you may be partially or fully phased out — check IRS thresholds for the year or calculate MAGI precisely. If you can claim up to $2,500, that reduces taxable income further (but likely you won’t be eligible given these numbers).

4) Standard deduction vs itemize
- 2024 standard deduction for a single filer: $14,600.
- Your known potential itemized deductions: charity $2,000 (you have a receipt). You haven’t listed mortgage interest, property taxes, or large SALT items. So itemizing (≈ $2,000) is well below the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction. The home‑office and business expenses reduce your self‑employment profit even if you take the standard deduction — they are above‑the‑line business deductions reported on Schedule C/Schedule 1 and are independent of the decision to itemize.

5) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest (if allowed) and certain other adjustments.
