Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I’m not a CPA — use this as a detailed checklist/estimate and consider confirming with a tax pro for filing or if you want exact tax calculations.

Quick summary of your numbers (so we’re working from the same base)
- W-2 wages (TechStart): $84,900
- Self-employed IT contracting income (gross): $15,000 (3 clients)
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + Charles Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Total gross income before business expenses/adjustments: $101,015

What you can deduct and how they are reported
1) Self‑employment/business expenses (Schedule C)
- Your 3-client IT contracting is self‑employment income. Report it on Schedule C.
- Deductible business expenses include the $650 unreimbursed expenses (assuming they’re ordinary and necessary for the contract work) and the business-use portion of your home (see below). Those reduce your Schedule C net profit.

2) Home‑office deduction (Schedule C + Form 8829 if using actual-expense method)
- You said the office is exclusive, 100 sq ft of a 690 sq ft home = 100/690 = ~14.49% business use.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Actual-expense: allocate your real housing cost (rent) proportionally. Your rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Business portion: 28,800 × 14.4927% ≈ $4,174. Because you rent, depreciation (for owners) is not applicable; the actual-expense method can include the rent portion and other allocable expenses. If you use actual-expense, you typically complete Form 8829 to compute the allowable home-office deduction.
- Important: The home-office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit and therefore reduces self-employment tax and income subject to withholding. If your Schedule C net loss exceeds other income limits, some deduction rules can limit the usable amount — but with $15k gross you’ll almost certainly have positive net profit.

3) Self‑employment tax and its deduction (Schedule SE / adjustment on Form 1040)
- You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C profit (SE tax is roughly 15.3% on 92.35% of net profit).
- You may deduct one-half of SE tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Example (approximate) using the actual-home method
- Home portion (actual): ≈ $4,174
- Business expenses (other): $650
- Schedule C net profit = 15,000 − 4,174 − 650 = $10,176
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 10,176 ≈ $1,438
- Deduction for half SE tax ≈ $719 (above-the-line)
- Net self-employment income that flows to Form 1040 = $10,176 (used in computing AGI), but adjustments lower AGI.

4) Student loan interest ($3,600 paid to MOHELA)
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction, capped at $2,500 per year (if you’re eligible).
- Eligibility is subject to a MAGI phase-out. For recent years the phase-out for single filers began well below $101k of income; with your combined income (~$101k before adjustments) you are likely above the upper phase-out threshold and therefore may not be able to claim the deduction in full (or at all). If you want, I can check exact MAGI thresholds for 2024 and do a precise phase-out estimate.
- If you are eligible, the maximum deduction is $2,500 — you cannot deduct the full $3,600 if eligible only up to that cap.

5) Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest)
- Cash contributions to qualified charities are deductible if you itemize on Schedule A.
- Because this is a cash gift of $250+, you need a contemporaneous written acknowledgement (receipt) from the charity to substantiate it.
- Note: If you use the standard deduction you cannot also deduct this donation (unless you do an advanced strategy such as bunching contributions).

6) Interest income
- The $920 and $195 should be reported (1099‑INT forms if issued). They increase gross income.

Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- For 2024 (inflation-adjusted), the standard deduction for single filers is $14,600. (Confirm with the IRS or your preparer if you want the latest official number.)
- Itemized deductions are primarily charitable contributions, state/local taxes (SALT — limited to $10k), mortgage interest, medical (above thresholds), etc. From the items you provided, the only Schedule A item appears to be the $2,000 charitable gift (you didn’t list SALT, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses).
- Conclusion: With only $2,000 of itemizable deductions listed, you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. The home‑office deduction does NOT go on Schedule A; it’s a business expense on Schedule C, so it does not affect the itemize-vs-standard decision.

Forms you’ll need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for reporting adjustments (e.g., student loan interest if eligible, one-half SE tax is an adjustment)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report your $15,000 contracting income and business expenses including unreimbursed expenses and home-office deduction (or the amount you compute from Form 8829).
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the deductible half of SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home office (not required for simplified method)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC — if clients issued them (you must report the income regardless of whether you received 1099s)
- 1099‑INTs for Chase and Schwab showing interest income
- Written acknowledgement (receipt) from Northwest Harvest for your $2,000 donation
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA — shows interest paid (you’ll need this to substantiate the student loan interest deduction, if you qualify)

Other practical tax notes
- Estimated taxes: Because you have self‑employment income and likely insufficient withholding for that portion, you may need to make estimated tax payments (quarterly) to avoid penalties. If you underpaid during the year, you could owe estimated tax and penalty.
- Retirement options: Consider contributing to a SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k) if you want to lower taxable income this year — you can reduce current tax and save for retirement. Those contributions are based on net self‑employment income and have deadlines (some with an extension). A tax pro can help calculate the exact deductible contribution.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for business expenses, donation acknowledgement for $2,000, 1098‑E, rent receipts/lease showing rent paid, and records of client payments and business-related expenses.

Rough tax-impact summary (estimate, not a final calculation)
- Net Schedule C profit (using actual home office): ≈ $10,176
- SE tax ≈ $1,438 (half deductible ≈ $719)
- Student loan interest: likely not deductible in full due to MAGI phaseout (you probably exceed the threshold with your income) — may be $0 allowable; if allowed, cap $2,500.
- Itemizing: unlikely to beat the standard deduction of $14,600 (so likely take the standard deduction).
If you’d like I will run a full, line-by-line estimate of your 2024 federal tax liability (showing AGI, taxable income, self-employment tax, and an estimate of the income tax due) — tell me whether you prefer the home‑office simplified ($500) or actual-expense ($4,174) method and whether you want me to assume you’re renting (you said rent) and whether you made any estimated tax payments during the year.

Next steps I can take for you
- Do a detailed tax estimate (I’ll show calculation lines and an estimate of federal tax and self-employment tax).
- Prepare a checklist of the documents to gather before you file or send to a preparer.
- Draft an email to a tax preparer with all the details and attach a summary of your numbers (if you want me to send or draft).

Which of those would you like me to do next?

