Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Below is a practical, step‑by‑step summary of how your 2024 return will look given the numbers you provided, plus recommendations and the forms you’ll likely need.

Quick facts you provided (I’ll use these in the examples below)
- Filing status: Single, San Diego, CA
- W‑2 wages (TechStart Inc): $84,900
- Self‑employment (IT contracting from 3 clients): $15,000
- Interest income: Chase savings $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Home: 690 sq ft; home office is 100 sq ft exclusive (100/690 = 14.49%)
- Rent: $2,400/month → $28,800/year
- Deductions/expenses: student loan interest paid $3,600 (2016–2019 loans with MOHELA), charitable donation $2,000 (receipt available), unreimbursed business expenses $650

1) What you can deduct (overview)
- Self‑employment business expenses (Schedule C):
  - Home office deduction (two choices — simplified or actual).
  - The $650 of unreimbursed expenses (if these are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your contracting work) — deductible on Schedule C.
  - Any other business expenses (supplies, software, phone portion used for business, advertising, mileage if applicable, etc.) if you have receipts.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income (reported on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Interest income: the $1,115 is taxable on Form 1040 (reported from 1099‑INTs).
- Charitable donation ($2,000): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct this on the federal return.
- Student loan interest ($3,600 paid): the student loan interest deduction is limited to a maximum of $2,500 and phases out with higher MAGI (modified adjusted gross income). See discussion below on whether you’ll qualify.

2) Home office: simplified vs actual method (which is better for you)
- Office percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.49% (about 14.5%).
- Simplified (safe-harbor): $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction.
- Actual method (allocate your home expenses): because you rent, you can allocate a portion of rent and utilities to the home office. Based on rent only:
  - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
  - Rent portion for office = 28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174.
  - If you have separate utility amounts, renter’s insurance, etc., you can pro rata allocate those too which increases the deduction.
- For your numbers the actual method (rent allocation) is much larger (~$4,174) than the simplified $500. So the actual method will generally be better unless you prefer the simplicity of the $500 calculation or you have no reliable records of other home expenses.

3) Rough numeric comparison and effect on AGI (estimates)
(These are approximate—used to show whether the student loan interest deduction is likely available and whether itemizing makes sense.)

Scenario A — actual home office method
- Business income: $15,000
- Business expenses: home office ≈ $4,174 + unreimbursed $650 = $4,824
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $15,000 − $4,824 = $10,176
- Self‑employment tax base ≈ 92.35% × 10,176 ≈ 9,400 → SE tax ≈ 9,400 × 15.3% ≈ $1,438
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $719
- Approx AGI = W‑2 (84,900) + interest (1,115) + net SE (10,176) − half SE tax (719) ≈ $95,472

Scenario B — simplified home office ($500)
- Business expenses = $500 + $650 = $1,150
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $13,850
- SE tax base ≈ 0.9235 × 13,850 ≈ 12,797 → SE tax ≈ $1,958; half ≈ $979
- Approx AGI = 84,900 + 1,115 + 13,850 − 979 ≈ $98,886

Implication for student loan interest deduction:
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 and it phases out at higher MAGI. With either scenario above, your estimated AGI is likely above the 2023 phaseout ceiling for singles; for 2024 the phaseout thresholds are inflation‑adjusted and you should check the IRS table, but given the estimates above you are likely above the upper phaseout and may not be able to claim the student loan interest deduction. Use the actual method for the home office (Scenario A) to lower AGI if you want the best chance of qualifying.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Your only listed itemized deductions are: charitable gift $2,000 (plus any state income taxes paid, property taxes, mortgage interest, etc., if any). You did not list mortgage interest or property taxes (you are renting), so likely your total itemizable deductions are small.
- The federal standard deduction for a single filer is substantially larger than $2,000 (check current IRS year table for the exact 2024 amount). Conclusion: you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on Form 1040 unless you have other deductible items you haven’t listed (significant mortgage interest, large state income tax paid, big medical expenses above the floor, much larger charitable gifts, etc.).

5) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (including either home office deduction or the simplified deduction).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual home‑expense method (you don’t need Form 8829 if you use the simplified $5/sq ft option).
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: you should receive 1099s from clients if they issued them; even if you don’t receive one you must report the income.
- Form 1099‑INT(s): interest income from banks (Chase, Schwab).
- Form 1098‑E: student loan interest statement from MOHELA (you will need this to claim the student loan interest deduction if eligible).
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on the numbers provided).
- California forms: California Form 540 (or the appropriate CA 1040-series form) — CA tax rules are similar but have differences; CA generally conforms with business‑use‑of‑home rules for the self-employed but check California tie‑ins.

6) Other important points
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties. Check whether you should pay estimated taxes for the year.
- Retirement accounts: as a self‑employed person you can reduce taxable income via retirement contributions (SEP‑IRA, solo 401(k)). Consider these options to reduce AGI and tax.
- Student loan interest paid in prior years (you listed 2016–2019): the $3,600 you list—make sure it is 2024 interest paid (only interest paid in the tax year is deductible). If those amounts refer to earlier years, they aren’t deductible now.
- Documentation: keep receipts for the $2,000 donation (acknowledgment if > $250), home office measurement and how you calculated allocation, rent receipts, utility bills (if using actual), 1099s, and records for the $650 expenses.

7) Practical recommendation
- Use the actual home office method (Form 8829) because rent × office % (≈ $4,174) is much larger than the $500 simplified deduction and will reduce your net self‑employment income and SE tax.
- Take the standard deduction on Form 1040 (unless you have significant additional itemizable deductions).
- Check the student loan interest MAGI phaseout for 2024 — with the numbers you’ve given, you are likely above the phaseout and may not be able to claim the student loan interest deduction; compute with exact figures (and any additional deductible retirement contributions) to be sure.
- Consider opening/contributing to a SEP‑IRA or solo 401(k) for 2024 if you want to reduce AGI and save for retirement — it can reduce taxable income and possibly preserve student loan interest eligibility if you’re near the cutoff.

8) If you want, I can:
- Run a more precise calculation if you give me any other itemizable items (state tax withheld / state income tax paid, mortgage interest, property tax, utility amounts, renter’s insurance, any other business expenses).
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect before you file.
- Draft a template email to your clients requesting 1099s or clarify payments (I can do that and send or draft it as an email for you).

Disclaimer: This is general information based on the numbers you provided and should not be interpreted as formal tax advice. For a definitive filing and to make year‑specific decisions (especially about student loan interest phaseouts and California specifics), consider confirming with a CPA or tax preparer.

Would you like me to:
- run a tighter estimate (I can re‑calculate if you provide any utility/renter’s insurance amounts or other expenses), or
- draft a documents checklist you can use to prepare your tax return?

