Done — I sent the information you gave to your tax contact (vince2000@gmail.com) with subject "User Tax Inquiry."

Below is a clear, practical summary of how your 2024 return would be handled, what you can likely deduct, which forms you’ll need, and the next steps/questions I need to finish a more precise calculation.

Quick facts you provided (used below)
- Filing status: Single, San Diego, CA
- W-2 wages: $84,900
- Self‑employment / contracting income: $15,000 (3 clients)
- Interest: Chase $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Rent: $2,400/month (690 sq ft home)
- Home office: 100 sq ft, regular & exclusive use
- Payments labeled “student loan interest” total $3,600 (MOHELA, 2016–2019) — see clarifying question below
- Charitable cash gifts: $2,000 (have receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $650

What to report and where (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- W-2 wages reported on Form 1040 (from TechStart).
- Interest (total $1,115) reported on Form 1040 interest line. (Schedule B only required if taxable interest > $1,500 — you are below that.)
- Self‑employment income:
  - Report gross receipts and business expenses on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - Calculate self‑employment tax on Schedule SE. You’ll owe Self‑Employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings and can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Home office:
  - Because you’re self‑employed, you can claim the home office deduction for the business portion of your rent and other qualifying home expenses.
  - Two methods:
    - Regular method: business % = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Deduct that percentage of rent and eligible utilities/insurance/repairs. Rent alone: $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,168. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate and then report the allowable amount on Schedule C.
    - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500. No Form 8829 required. Simpler but yields less in your case (regular method likely gives a larger deduction because your rent is high).
- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Schedule 1) up to $2,500, subject to MAGI phaseouts. You cannot deduct more than the annual cap.
  - IMPORTANT CLARIFICATION NEEDED: The deduction only applies to interest you paid in the tax year (2024). You listed “MOHELA, 2016–2019” and $3,600 — do you mean you paid $3,600 during 2024, or are those years when the loans were originated or accrued? If the $3,600 wasn’t paid in 2024, it is not deductible on your 2024 return. Also you should have Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for interest paid in 2024.
- Charitable contributions:
  - $2,000 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it on the federal return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses:
  - If these $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses (ordinary W‑2 job costs), they are NOT deductible on your federal return through 2025 (suspended by TCJA).
  - If they are business expenses related to your contracting work, include them on Schedule C.
- State (California):
  - You’ll file CA Form 540. California generally starts from federal AGI but has differences (e.g., limited deductions). Check CA guidance or a tax pro for any CA-specific adjustments, but the basic reporting (W‑2, Schedule C income) flows to CA return.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- The choice is: itemize on Schedule A (and include charitable $2,000, state & local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, medical >7.5% AGI if any, mortgage interest if any) OR take the standard deduction.
- For a single filer, the standard deduction is typically substantial (likely larger than $2,000 alone). Given the items you listed (only $2,000 charity plus SALT that may or may not push you over the standard deduction), you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable expenses (large state tax withholding/payments, property tax, mortgage interest, or large unreimbursed medical bills).
- Practical step: add up (a) your charitable donations + (b) state and local taxes you actually paid in 2024 (income tax withheld + any state estimated payments + property tax) + (c) other itemizable amounts. If that total > standard deduction, itemize; otherwise take standard.

Estimated tax & self‑employment tax
- Because you have self‑employment income, you’re subject to SE tax (~15.3% on net self‑employment earnings, with the 0.9235 adjustment). You may also need to make quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties if you expect to owe $1,000+ when filing.
- You may reduce taxable income by the deductible half of SE tax (adjustment on Schedule 1).

Other forms/documents you’ll need / want to collect
- W-2 from TechStart.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if clients issued) or your own records of the $15,000 receipts.
- 1099‑INT(s) from Chase and Schwab (or bank statements).
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest paid in 2024 (if applicable).
- Receipts for charitable contribution(s) (you said you have them).
- Records/receipts for business expenses (including the $650 if business related), and documentation supporting home office (floorplan showing exclusive space, rent receipts, utility bills) — keep these for your records.
- Form 8829 only if you use the regular home‑office method.
- Schedule C and Schedule SE for business/self‑employment.

Rough illustration (example) — not an exact calculation, just to show mechanics
- If your net self‑employment earnings after allowable business expenses (including home office) are roughly $10,000, your SE tax might be in the ballpark of $1,400–$1,500 and half of that (~$700–$750) is deductible on Schedule 1. That reduces AGI and can affect whether you can claim the full student loan interest deduction (subject to MAGI limits).
- Using the regular home office method appears likely to give a larger deduction than the simplified method (approx. $4,168 vs $500 for rent alone), but you should total all eligible home expenses first.

Action items / questions for you
1. Did you actually pay $3,600 in student loan interest during 2024? Or are those interest amounts for prior years? (You should have a 1098‑E for interest paid in 2024 — do you have it?)
2. Are the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses (related to your contracting) or unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job? If business, include on Schedule C; if employee, they are not deductible on the federal return for 2024.
3. Do you receive any 1099‑NEC forms from your clients, or did they not issue 1099s? (You must report the $15,000 even if no 1099s were issued.)
4. Do you pay property tax or large state estimated payments (these affect itemizing and SALT cap)?
5. Any other business expenses (equipment, software, subscriptions, mileage, phone, supplies)?

Recommendations
- Gather your W‑2, any 1099s, 1098‑E (student loan interest), bank statements / 1099‑INT, and receipts for business/home office/charitable donations.
- Use the regular home office method (Form 8829) if your rent/utility share produces a bigger deduction (it likely will here).
- Plan to make quarterly estimated tax payments for 2025 if you expect similar self‑employment income.
- If you want, I can:
  - Run a quick estimate of your federal tax and whether itemizing beats the standard deduction if you give me: exact state tax withheld (from your W‑2), exact amount of student loan interest paid in 2024 (and confirm you have 1098‑E), and whether the $650 is self‑employment expense.
  - Draft an email / checklist you can send to a tax preparer with all the items you need to provide.

Would you like me to (pick one):
- Run a numeric estimate now (I’ll need the state tax withheld on your W‑2, exact student loan interest paid in 2024, confirmation whether $650 is business expense, and any other business deductions), OR
- Prepare a printable checklist/email you can send to your preparer, OR
- Walk through filling Schedule C / Form 8829 step‑by‑step with your actual expense numbers?

If you want a numeric estimate, please answer questions 1–3 above and give the state tax withheld from your W‑2.

